(function(){if(!window.__hermes_register_book)return;window.__hermes_register_book("simonides-fragments",[{"st": "—", "sp": "HDR", "t": "The Fragments of Simonides"}, {"st": "—", "sp": "N", "t": "<figure class=\"reader-figure\"><img class=\"reader-figure-img\" src=\"data:image/jpeg;base64,\" alt=\"\" loading=\"lazy\"><figcaption>A 1900 depiction of the Battle of Marathon, which Simonides was commissioned to commemorate<\/figcaption><\/figure>"}, {"st": "—", "sp": "HDR", "t": "FRAGMENTS CONCERNING THE LIFE OF SIMONIDES"}, {"st": "—", "sp": "N", "t": "<span class=\"sm-src\">Strabo Geography<\/span>CEOS was originally a tetrapolis, but only two of the four remain as separate cities, Iulis and Carthaea, the former having absorbed Coresia and the latter Poieëssa. Iulis was the birthplace of the lyric poet Simonides and of his nephew Bacchylides, and later of the physician Erasistratus and the Peripatetic philosopher Ariston... There appears to have been a law here, mentioned by Menander in the lines ‘The Cean custom takes my fancy still, / The man who can’t live well shall not live ill,’ whereby, in order to make the supplies go round, all citizens who had reached the age of sixty should drink the hemlock. [hence partly perh. S.’s voluntary exile after middle-age; had the law been enforced in his case, much of his finest extant work would never have been done]"}, {"st": "—", "sp": "N", "t": "<span class=\"sm-src\">Herodotus Histories<\/span>Following upon their track, the Persians came up with the flying Ionians at Ephesus, and when they turned and showed fight inflicted upon them a severe defeat, after which they put to the sword a number of well-known men, including the Eretrian commander Eualcides, who had taken the prize at crown-contests in the Games and been highly eulogised by Simonides of Ceos."}, {"st": "—", "sp": "N", "t": "<span class=\"sm-src\">[Plato] Hipparchus<\/span>... Hipparchus, the eldest and wisest of the sons of Peisistratus, who among other fine ways showed his wisdom... in inducing Simonides of Ceos by high pay and valuable presents to be in continual attendance upon him."}, {"st": "—", "sp": "N", "t": "<span class=\"sm-src\">Aelian Historical Miscellanies<\/span>Leoprepes of Ceos, the father of Simonides, was sitting one day in a wrestling school, when some boys who had formed mutual friendships asked, the grown-up man how they could best make their friendship last; to which he replied: ‘By making allowance for one another’s dispositions instead of rousing one another’s anger by a challenge of spirit.’"}, {"st": "—", "sp": "N", "t": "<span class=\"sm-src\">Parian Chronicle<\/span>From the time when the Ceian Simonides son of Leoprepes, the inventor of the system of memory-aids, won the chorus-prize at Athens, and the statues were set up to Harmodius and Aristogeiton, 213 years (B.C. 477)."}, {"st": "—", "sp": "N", "t": "<span class=\"sm-src\">Suidas Lexicon<\/span>Simonides (1st notice): — Son of Leoprepes, of Iulis, a city of the island of Ceos; a lyric poet; coming next to Stesichorus; called, because of the sweetness of his style, Melicertes; originator of the art of mnemonics. He also invented the signs for the long vowels, H and Ω, and the double letters Ξ and Ψ, [as well as the third note on the lyre]. He was born in the 56th Olympiad (B.C. 556-553) — or according to some accounts in the 62nd (532-529) — and lived till the 78th (468 — 465), attaining the age of eighty-nine. He wrote the following works in the Doric dialect: — The Kingdom of Cambyses and Darius, The Sea-fight with Xerxes, The Sea fight of Artemisium, these in elegiacs; The Sea-fight at Salamis in lyric metre; Dirges, Eulogies, Inscriptions, Paeans, Tragedies, etc. This Simonides had a very remarkable memory..."}, {"st": "—", "sp": "N", "t": "<span class=\"sm-src\">Life of Aeschylus<\/span>According to some authorities, Aeschylus was defeated by Simonides in the competition for an elegy on those who fell at Marathon. For the elegiac metre requires the fineness of detail which is associated with the rousing of sympathy, and that, as we have said, is foreign to Aeschylus."}, {"st": "—", "sp": "N", "t": "<span class=\"sm-src\">Aristophanes Birds<\/span>POET: I’ve written some lyrics to your Cloudcuckooborough, a lot of fine dithyrambs and some maiden-songs, and — you know, the Simonides trick."}, {"st": "—", "sp": "N", "t": "<span class=\"sm-src\">The Same Wasps (see on Lasus p. 223).The Same Peace (Hermes and Trygaeus)<\/span>H. She (Peace) first asked after Sophocles.T. He’s all right; but there’s something remarkable happening to him.H. What’s that?T. He’s changing into Simonides.H. Simonides? What d’ye mean?T. I mean that now that he’s old and off colour he’d go to sea on a hurdle to earn a groat."}, {"st": "—", "sp": "N", "t": "<span class=\"sm-src\">Scholiast on the passage<\/span>Simonides seems to have been the first to connect poetry with meanness of disposition and to write it for pay; which is what Pindar hints at in his Isthmians (2. 10), where he says, ‘For the Muse was no seeker of gain then, nor worked for hire....’"}, {"st": "—", "sp": "N", "t": "<span class=\"sm-src\">Scholiast on the passage of Pindar<\/span>He means that nowadays they compose victory-songs for pay, a custom begun by Simonides..."}, {"st": "—", "sp": "N", "t": "<span class=\"sm-src\">Plutarch Should Old Men Govern?<\/span>Simonides said to the friends who accused him of penuriousness, that the pleasure of making profit was the one and only pleasure he had left to tend him in his old age."}, {"st": "—", "sp": "N", "t": "<span class=\"sm-src\">Stobaeus Anthology<\/span>When Simonides was asked why at his advanced age he was so careful of his money, he replied, ‘It is because I should rather leave money for enemies when I die than stand in need of friends while I live; for I know too well how few friendships last.’"}, {"st": "—", "sp": "N", "t": "<span class=\"sm-src\">Plato Republic<\/span>‘But still,’ said I, ‘I find it difficult to disbelieve a great and inspired artist like Simonides.’"}, {"st": "—", "sp": "N", "t": "<span class=\"sm-src\">From a Papyrus of the 3rd Century B.C.<\/span>On Expenditure: — Simonides: The following sayings of his are also esteemed for their truth to nature. When asked by the wife of Hiero if all things grew old, he replied ‘Yes, all, except love of gain; and acts of kindness sooner than anything else.’ Again, when he was asked why he was so penurious, he answered that it was because he got more vexation from debit than from credit; either was really negligible, though both derived importance from the passions and unreasonableness of men; and so neither of them did him any harm, or, strictly speaking, any good; but it was irksome to use another man’s staff instead of one’s own; moreover, borrowed money might cost little at the moment, but in the end it cost twice as much; and so we ought to count every penny. Lastly he declared that when he consumed only the necessary and natural food of man, simple food like that of the animals, he was borrowing from himself."}, {"st": "—", "sp": "N", "t": "<span class=\"sm-src\">Introduction to Theocritus 16 (cf. 1. 10)<\/span>The story goes that Simonides kept two boxes, the one for fees and the other for favours; and whenever any friend came asking a favour, he had the boxes brought in and opened before him, and cut short his importunity by discovering the favour-box to be empty and the fee-box full. [cf. Stob. Flor. 10. 39 where the story is told of a man who asked for a eulogy and said he would take it as a favour]"}, {"st": "—", "sp": "N", "t": "<span class=\"sm-src\">Theocritus<\/span>Many indeed were the bondmen earned their monthly meed in the houses of Antiochus and King Aleuas, many the calves that went lowing with the horned kine home to byres of the Scopads, and ten thousand were the fine sheep that the shepherds of the plain of Crannon watched all night for the hospitable Creondae; but once all the sweet wine of their life was in the great cup, once they were embarked in the barge of the old man loathsome, the joyance and pleasure of those things was theirs no more: and though they left behind them all that great and noble wealth, they had lain among the vile dead long ages unremembered, had not the great Ceian cried sweet varied lays to the strings and famoused them in posterity, and had not the coursers that came home to them victorious out of the Games achieved the honour and glory which called the poet to his task."}, {"st": "—", "sp": "N", "t": "<span class=\"sm-src\">Scholiast on the passage<\/span>... By this he implies the possession of great riches, so as to be able to feed many retainers. ‘But all the same,’ says he, ‘their wealth would have been of no avail for the preservation of their glory, had their praises not been sung by Simonides.’... The Scopads were Crannonians by birth, and Crannon is a city of Thessaly, birthplace of Scopas son of Creon and Echecrateia. Compare Simonides in the Dirges... By ‘the great Ceian’ he means Simonides, who wrote victory-songs and dirges for the aforesaid great Thessalians."}, {"st": "—", "sp": "N", "t": "<span class=\"sm-src\">Plutarch On Listening to Poetry<\/span>And that is why, when they asked Simonides why the Thessalians were the only people he never cheated, he replied ‘They are too great dunces.’"}, {"st": "—", "sp": "N", "t": "<span class=\"sm-src\">Diogenes Laertius Lives of the Philosophers<\/span>According to Aristotle in the 3rd Book of his Treatise on Poetry (fr. 65)... Antimenidas and Alcaeus had a feud with Pittacus, Sosibius with Anaxagoras, and Timocreon with Simonides."}, {"st": "—", "sp": "N", "t": "<span class=\"sm-src\">Plato Republic (Socrates and Polemarchus)<\/span>What do you say, said I, that Simonides rightly says about justice? — That justice is to give every man his due."}, {"st": "—", "sp": "N", "t": "<span class=\"sm-src\">Stobaeus Anthology<\/span>Simonides: — When a friend of his once told Simonides that he was hearing a great deal of slander about him, he replied ‘Be so good as to stop defaming me with your ears.’"}, {"st": "—", "sp": "N", "t": "<span class=\"sm-src\">Plutarch Garrulity<\/span>In all things and for all these reasons we ought to bear in mind the saying of Simonides, that he had often repented speaking but had never repented holding his tongue."}, {"st": "—", "sp": "N", "t": "<span class=\"sm-src\">Plutarch Dinner-Table Problems<\/span>One evening over the wine, when the poet Simonides saw a guest sitting absolutely silent, he exclaimed ‘If you’re a fool, my good sir, you’re wise in what you do; and if you’re wise, you’re a fool.’"}, {"st": "—", "sp": "N", "t": "<span class=\"sm-src\">The Same The Glory of Athens<\/span>Simonides calls ‘painting silent poetry and poetry painting that speaks’; for the actions which painters depict as they are being performed, words describe after they are done."}, {"st": "—", "sp": "N", "t": "<span class=\"sm-src\">Aristotle in Stobaeus Anthology<\/span>We are told that when Simonides was asked what was meant by good birth, he replied ‘ancestral wealth.’"}, {"st": "—", "sp": "N", "t": "<span class=\"sm-src\">Michael Psellus The Function of Daemons<\/span>... According to Simonides the word is the image of the thing."}, {"st": "—", "sp": "N", "t": "<span class=\"sm-src\">Aristides On the Extempore Addition [how epitaphs prove that states are guilty of self-praise]<\/span>So you may laugh at the fallen [for praising themselves] and call them underground babblers who cannot keep still; and some disciple of Simonides will retort ‘you are more dead above ground, my good man, than those are below it.’"}, {"st": "—", "sp": "N", "t": "<span class=\"sm-src\">Theon First Course in Grammar<\/span>... Simonides gives harmful advice when he says we should play all our lives and never be entirely in earnest."}, {"st": "—", "sp": "N", "t": "<span class=\"sm-src\">Aristotle Physics<\/span>In time everything comes into existence and passes out of it; and that is why some writers called Time wisest of things; though Paron the Pythagorean says it is foolishest, because we also forget in it — which indeed is more correct."}, {"st": "—", "sp": "N", "t": "<span class=\"sm-src\">Ath. 2. 40 a [on wine]<\/span>Simonides ascribes wine and music to one and the same origin."}, {"st": "—", "sp": "N", "t": "<span class=\"sm-src\">Plutarch Consolation to Apollonius<\/span>One day when the Spartan king Pausanias, who was suffering from swelled head, hade the lyrist Simonides tell him a wise tale with a jest in it, the poet, fully appreciating the king’s conceited humour, advised him to remember that he was human."}, {"st": "—", "sp": "N", "t": "<span class=\"sm-src\">The Same Life of Themistocles<\/span>All the same he ingratiated himself with the people by calling every citizen by his name, and by putting his sure judgment at their disposal in matters of business. Indeed, when Simonides of Ceos made an improper request of him during the time of his command, he retorted that he would not be a good minister of state if he put favour before law, any more than Simonides would be a good poet if he sang out of tune."}, {"st": "—", "sp": "N", "t": "<span class=\"sm-src\">Aristotle Rhetoric [on wealth]<\/span>Thus when Simonides was speaking of wisdom and riches with the wife of Hiero, and she asked him which was better, to get wise or to get wealthy, he replied ‘To get wealthy; for I see the wise sitting on the doorsteps of the rich.’"}, {"st": "—", "sp": "N", "t": "<span class=\"sm-src\">Cicero The Nature of the Gods<\/span>If you were to ask me the nature and attributes of God, I should reply in the words of Simonides, who when asked this very question by the despot Hiero asked for a day’s notice of it, and when it was repeated the next day, requested two days more, and so on, doubling every time, till at last, when the wondering Hiero asked him to explain his strange behaviour, he replied that it was because the longer he thought about it, the more obscure it became. I believe that the truth is that Simonides, of whom tradition speaks not only as a delightful poet but in all respects a wise and learned man, despaired of the true answer because so many subtle definitions occurred to him that he could not decide among them."}, {"st": "—", "sp": "N", "t": "<span class=\"sm-src\">Athenaeus Doctors at Dinner<\/span>If we may believe Chamaeleon, Simonides’ niggardliness is a real fact. For instance at Syracuse, where Hiero was in the habit of sending him daily a portion of food, Simonides used openly to sell most of it and keep only a small part for himself; and once, when he was asked the reason, replied that he did so as a testimony to Hiero’s munificence and his own moderation."}, {"st": "—", "sp": "N", "t": "<span class=\"sm-src\">Plutarch Life of Aratus<\/span>Even if it be a terrible thins: to deal thus with one’s fellow-clansmen and kinsfolk by anger, still in Simonides’ words ‘In time of necessity even harshness is sweet,’ healing as it were and restoring the spirit when it is sick and fevered."}, {"st": "—", "sp": "N", "t": "<span class=\"sm-src\">Timaeus quoted by the Scholiast on Pindar<\/span>Thus Theron took umbrage on behalf both of his daughter (Demaretè) and her husband (Polyzelus), and made war upon Polyzelus’ brother Hiero, advancing to the Sicilian river Gela.... But not a blow was struck, and the war came to nothing. For we are told that the lyric poet Simonides came up in the nick of time and reconciled the two kings."}, {"st": "—", "sp": "N", "t": "<span class=\"sm-src\">Aristotle Metaphysics<\/span>All these things are irrational, and inconsistent both with one another and with what is rational, and we may apply to them the term ‘a long story’ as it is used by Simonides, a long story in that sense being the kind of account given by a slave when he will not talk sense."}, {"st": "—", "sp": "N", "t": "<span class=\"sm-src\">Alexander of Aphrodisias on the passage<\/span>These words will be clear to any reader who has been told what is meant by the λογος of Simonides. This writer, in what he calls his ‘Aτακτοί Λόγοι or Prose Conversations, [meaning doubtful; apparently some sort of Mime] imitates [that is, gives a literary representation of] the answers erring slaves will generally make when their masters are enquiring why they have blundered, and makes them give extremely long and verbose excuses which have no sense — that is, plausibility — but are entirely off the point. This would seem to be characteristic of foreign birth and lack of education."}, {"st": "—", "sp": "N", "t": "<span class=\"sm-src\">Pindar Olympians<\/span>Skilled is the man who knoweth much by nature; they that have but learnt — even as a pair of crows, gluttonous in their wordiness, these chatter vain things against the divine bird of Zeus."}, {"st": "—", "sp": "N", "t": "<span class=\"sm-src\">Scholiast on the passage<\/span>He hints at Bacchylides and Simonides, calling himself an eagle and his rivals crows."}, {"st": "—", "sp": "N", "t": "<span class=\"sm-src\">Scholiast on Pindar<\/span>... Simonides often employs digression."}, {"st": "—", "sp": "N", "t": "<span class=\"sm-src\">Eustathius Introduction to the Commentary on Pindar<\/span>It is said that Pindar was a pupil also of Simonides."}, {"st": "—", "sp": "N", "t": "<span class=\"sm-src\">Plutarch Music<\/span>Pancrates usually avoided the chromatic scale, though he used it occasionally, thus showing that he did not avoid it from ignorance. Indeed he tells us himself that he imitates the musical style of Pindar and Simonides and, generally, what is now called the ancient style."}, {"st": "—", "sp": "N", "t": "<span class=\"sm-src\">Longinus the Rhetorician<\/span>Simonides and many after him have pointed out paths to remembrance, counselling us to compare images and localities in order to remember names and events, but there is nothing more in it than the concatenation and coobservation of the apparently new with what is similar to it."}, {"st": "—", "sp": "N", "t": "<span class=\"sm-src\">Cicero On the Chief Good and the Chief Evil<\/span>Is it in our power to remember or to forget? When Themistocles was once promised — by Simonides I think it was — a handbook of mnemonics or guide to remembrance, ‘I should prefer,’ he exclaimed, ‘a guide to forgetfulness; for I remember things I would not and cannot forget what I would.’"}, {"st": "—", "sp": "N", "t": "<span class=\"sm-src\">Scholiast on Dionysius of Thrace [on the Alphabet]<\/span>As for the inventors of the remaining eight letters, namely the two long vowels, the three double letters, and the three aspirates, it is clear that Simonides of Ceos invented the H, the Ω, the Ξ and the Ψ, and Palamedes the Θ, the Φ, the X, and the Z, though some authorities ascribe these last four (?) to Epicharmus of Syracuse."}, {"st": "—", "sp": "N", "t": "Plutarch Should Old Men Govern? Simonides won the chorus prize in his old age.Lucian Longevity: Simonides of Ceos lived to be over ninety."}, {"st": "—", "sp": "N", "t": "<span class=\"sm-src\">Suidas Lexicon<\/span>Simonides (3rd notice): ... There was an Agrigentine General named Phoenix who, in the course of a war with Syracuse, with cynical ruthlessness pulled down the tomb of Simonides and made a bastion of the stones. At that spot the city was taken. [context implies Acragas; S. then apparently died there and not at Syracuse] This story would seem to be confirmed by Callimachus of Cyrene, who deplores the outrage and makes this writer of delightful verse say: ‘neither had he respect for the writing thereon which declared that beneath lay the son of Leoprepes of Ceos’; and a little further on: ‘nor yet had he any fear of you brethren, O Polydeuces, who made me, alone of all the guests, pass out ere the roof fell, when the house at Crannon came down alas! upon the mighty Scopadae."}, {"st": "—", "sp": "N", "t": "<span class=\"sm-src\">Athenaeus Doctors at Dinner<\/span>Compare the author of the play called The Helots: ‘It’s old-fashioned to sing Stesichorus, Alcman, or Simonides; but we can hear Gnesippus..."}, {"st": "—", "sp": "N", "t": "<span class=\"sm-src\">Scholiast on Aristophanes Wasps [‘mind you take up the catch properly’]<\/span>It was an old custom for guests at table to continue where the first singer left off. The guest who began held a sprig of bay or myrtle and sang a lyric of Simonides or Stesichorus as far as he chose, and then handed the sprig to another, making his choice of a successor with no regard to the order in which the guests were seated. His successor then continued the song, and in turn passed on the sprig at his own caprice."}, {"st": "—", "sp": "N", "t": "<span class=\"sm-src\">Athenaeus Doctors at Dinner<\/span>... according to Chamaeleon in his treatise On Simonides."}, {"st": "—", "sp": "N", "t": "<span class=\"sm-src\">Suidas Lexicon<\/span>Palaephatus: — An Egyptian, or according to some authorities, an Athenian; grammarian; wrote Arguments or introductions to the works of Simonides."}, {"st": "—", "sp": "N", "t": "<span class=\"sm-src\">Palatine Anthology<\/span>The Garland of Meleager: ... and a fresh young spray of the vine-buds of Simonides."}, {"st": "—", "sp": "N", "t": "<span class=\"sm-src\">Catullus<\/span>... a word of talk as sad as the tears of Simonides."}, {"st": "—", "sp": "N", "t": "<span class=\"sm-src\">Suidas Lexicon<\/span>Tryphon: — Son of Ammonius; of Alexandria; grammarian and poet; flourished in the reign of Augustus and earlier; wrote... on the Dialects in Homer and in Simonides, Pindar, Alcman, and the other lyric poets."}, {"st": "—", "sp": "N", "t": "<span class=\"sm-src\">Dionysius of Halicarnassus Critique of the Ancient Writers<\/span>— You should note in Simonides his choice of words and his nicety in combining them; moreover — and here he surpasses even Pindar — he is remarkable for his expression of pity not by employing the grand style but by appealing to the emotions."}, {"st": "—", "sp": "N", "t": "<span class=\"sm-src\">Quintilian Guide to Oratory [the Nine Lyric Poets]<\/span>Simonides, though in other respects not a commanding figure, may be praised for his choice of expression and for a certain sweetness; but his chief excellence lies in his pathos; indeed some critics consider that in this quality he surpasses all other writers of this class of literature."}, {"st": "—", "sp": "HDR", "t": "THE POEMS OF SIMONIDES"}, {"st": "—", "sp": "HDR", "t": "BOOK I. HYMNS"}, {"st": "—", "sp": "HDR", "t": "To Olympian Zeus"}, {"st": "1", "sp": "N", "t": "<span class=\"sm-src\">Himerius Declamations<\/span><span class=\"sm-ctx\">One day when Simonides was hastening to Pisa to honour Zeus with a hymn, the Eleans laid hold of his lyre and all with one accord bade him sing the city instead of Zeus.<\/span>"}, {"st": "—", "sp": "HDR", "t": "To Poseidon"}, {"st": "2", "sp": "N", "t": "<span class=\"sm-src\">Scholiast on Euripides Medea [‘the all-golden pelt’]<\/span><span class=\"sm-ctx\">The fleece, which according to some authorities was entirely of gold, according to others of purple. Simonides in the Hymn to Poseidon says that it was dyed in sea-purple.<\/span>"}, {"st": "—", "sp": "N", "t": "<span class=\"sm-src\">Etymologicum Magnum<\/span><span class=\"sm-ctx\">νάκη means a goatskin, κωδία and κώδιον a sheepskin. And so the Colchian <i class=\"sm-qi\">fleece<\/i> ought not to be called νακος, and Simonides is wrong in this.<\/span>"}, {"st": "—", "sp": "N", "t": "<span class=\"sm-src\">Scholiast on Apollonius of Rhodes Argonautica<\/span><span class=\"sm-ctx\">Many have made the Fleece golden.... Simonides sometimes calls it white and sometimes purple.<\/span>"}, {"st": "—", "sp": "N", "t": "<span class=\"sm-note\">[seems to imply that S. mentioned the Fleece in more poems than one; cf. Tzet. Chü. 1. 430 where S. is said to have applied the epithet to the lamb of Atreus]<\/span>"}, {"st": "3", "sp": "N", "t": "<span class=\"sm-src\">Scholiast on Euripides Medea [‘the Clashing Rocks’]<\/span><span class=\"sm-ctx\">These are called by Simonides <i class=\"sm-qi\">the Together-moving Rocks<\/i><\/span>"}, {"st": "4", "sp": "N", "t": "<span class=\"sm-ctx\">Scholiast on Pindar [... ‘Lemnos, where the Argonauts gave proof of their limbs in a contest for a garment (or without their clothes),]... And indeed in Simonides’ account the clothing is the prize.<\/span>"}, {"st": "5", "sp": "N", "t": "<span class=\"sm-src\">Scholiast on Euripides Medea<\/span><span class=\"sm-ctx\">We are told that Medea was queen of Corinth by Eumelus, and by Simonides in the lines:<\/span>"}, {"st": "—", "sp": "N", "t": "<span class=\"sm-frag\">And when Jason returned he dwelt not at Magnesia but at Corinth, and ruled Cranum and Lechaeum as hearth-fellow of a Colchian spouse.<\/span>"}, {"st": "6", "sp": "N", "t": "<span class=\"sm-src\">Scholiast on Apollonius of Rhodes Argonautica<\/span><span class=\"sm-ctx\">The marriage of Medea to Achilles on his arrival in the Elysian Plain is first told by Ibycus, and after him by Simonides.<\/span>"}, {"st": "7", "sp": "N", "t": "<span class=\"sm-src\">Introduction to Euripides Medea<\/span><span class=\"sm-ctx\">According to Pherecydes and Simonides, Medea made Jason young again by boiling him.<\/span>"}, {"st": "—", "sp": "HDR", "t": "BOOK II. PAEANS"}, {"st": "8", "sp": "N", "t": "<span class=\"sm-src\">Himerius Declamations<\/span><span class=\"sm-ctx\">Therefore I believe what Simonides says in praise of the Muses in his Lyric poems:<\/span>"}, {"st": "—", "sp": "N", "t": "<span class=\"sm-frag\">A dance is ever dear to the Muses, but when they espy Apollo about to lead a round, then more than ever put they forth their best in music and send down Helicon an all-harmonious sound.<\/span>"}, {"st": "9", "sp": "N", "t": "<span class=\"sm-src\">Julian Letters<\/span><span class=\"sm-ctx\">It suffices the lyrist Simonides for praise of Apollo to call the God ‘Εκατος or<\/span>"}, {"st": "—", "sp": "N", "t": "<span class=\"sm-frag\">Far-Shooter<\/span>"}, {"st": "—", "sp": "N", "t": "<span class=\"sm-ctx\">and to adorn him with that title instead, as it were, of another sacred mark — because, as he says, he slew the serpent Python with a hundred (εκατόν) arrows.<\/span>"}, {"st": "—", "sp": "HDR", "t": "BOOK III. PRAYERS"}, {"st": "10", "sp": "N", "t": "<span class=\"sm-src\">Plutarch Political Precepts<\/span><span class=\"sm-ctx\">But since, as Simonides says:Every lark must have his crest<\/span>"}, {"st": "—", "sp": "N", "t": "<span class=\"sm-frag\">and every form of government involves some hatred and strife, it is particularly necessary for the statesman to have studied these things.<\/span>"}, {"st": "11", "sp": "N", "t": "<span class=\"sm-src\">Scholiast on the Odyssey [‘For I went to Delos with a large company’]<\/span><span class=\"sm-ctx\">This refers not to his own people but to the Grecian expedition as a whole, at the head of which Menelaus went to Delos with Odysseus after the daughters of Anius who were called the Turners-into-Wine. The story is given by Simonides in the Prayers.<\/span>"}, {"st": "—", "sp": "HDR", "t": "On the Sea-fight off Artemisium"}, {"st": "12-16", "sp": "N", "t": "<span class=\"sm-src\">Scholiast on Apollonius of Rhodes Argonautica. [‘Thither came also Zetes and Calais, sons of the Northwind]<\/span><span class=\"sm-ctx\">...<\/span>"}, {"st": "—", "sp": "N", "t": "<span class=\"sm-ctx\">According to Simonides, Oreithyia was carried off from Brilessus to the Sarpedonian Rock in Thrace... Oreithyia was the daughter of Erechtheus whom the Northwind carried off from Attica to Thrace, there to beget on her Zetes and Calais, as Simonides tells in the Sea-Fight.<\/span>"}, {"st": "13,14", "sp": "N", "t": "<span class=\"sm-src\">Priscian Metres of Terence<\/span><span class=\"sm-ctx\">Simonides and Alcman, according to Heliodorus, not only end iambic lines with spondees, but put them [irregularly] elsewhere. Simonides in the Sea-Fight off Artemisinin puts a spondee in the second place in the catalectic dimeter:<\/span>"}, {"st": "—", "sp": "N", "t": "<span class=\"sm-frag\">the sea began to roar,<br>corresponding in the antistrophe to<br>they avert the Death-Goddesses<\/span>"}, {"st": "15", "sp": "N", "t": "<span class=\"sm-src\">Himerius Declamations<\/span><span class=\"sm-ctx\">A song shall loose the moorings of the vessel, the song which the holy chorus of Athenians sings to call the wind to the ship, that it may be present indeed and fly along with the sacred craft;[the sacred ship in the Panathenaic procession] and the wind, doubtless recognising the song which Simonides sang to it after the sea ⟨[-fight⟩, comes straightway at the call of the music, and blowing strong and full astern drives the bark on with its blast.<\/span>"}, {"st": "—", "sp": "N", "t": "<span class=\"sm-src\">The Same Eclogues<\/span><span class=\"sm-ctx\">For now desiring to call the wind in poetic wise, but being unable to utter poetic speech, I would fain call the wind according to the Ceian Muse... and softly spreading over the waves, cleave thou the purple waves about the bows; for in quest of no wicked desires would he sail thy sea, but in the hope of initiating all Ionia into the holy mysteries of virtuous living.<\/span>"}, {"st": "16", "sp": "N", "t": "<span class=\"sm-src\">Scholiast on Apollonius of Rhodes Argonautica [‘Sea-girt Sciathos’]<\/span><span class=\"sm-ctx\">Sciathos is an island of Thessaly close to Euboea; it is mentioned by Simonides.<\/span>"}, {"st": "—", "sp": "HDR", "t": "BOOK IV. DITHYRAMBS"}, {"st": "—", "sp": "HDR", "t": "Memnon"}, {"st": "17", "sp": "N", "t": "<span class=\"sm-src\">Strabo Geography [on Susa]<\/span><span class=\"sm-ctx\">It is said to have been founded by Tithonus father of Memnon... The acropolis was called the Memnonium, and the Susians are known as Cissian, a title which Aeschylus gives to the mother of Memnon; moreover Memnon is said to have been buried near Paltus in Syria, on the banks of the river Badas, as is told by Simonides in his Dithyramb Memnon included among the Deliaca.<\/span>"}, {"st": "—", "sp": "HDR", "t": "Europa"}, {"st": "18", "sp": "N", "t": "<span class=\"sm-src\">Aristophanes of Byzantium<\/span><span class=\"sm-ctx\">In his Europa Simonides calls the bull not only a bull but also μηλον and πρόβατον [which usually are applied to sheep or goats.]<\/span>"}, {"st": "—", "sp": "HDR", "t": "BOOK V. EULOGIES"}, {"st": "—", "sp": "HDR", "t": "To Scopas"}, {"st": "19", "sp": "N", "t": "<span class=\"sm-note\">[Plato does not quote the rest of this stanza; if the poem is complete except for these lines and a line and a half below, this gap doubtless contained the personal application of the general statement to Scopas]<\/span>"}, {"st": "—", "sp": "N", "t": "<span class=\"sm-src\">Plato Protagoras [beginning a fall discussion of the poem, whence this is reconstructed]<\/span><span class=\"sm-ctx\">For does not Simonides say to Scopas son of Creon the Thessalian:<\/span>"}, {"st": "—", "sp": "N", "t": "<span class=\"sm-frag\">It is hard to quit you like a truly good man fashioned without flaw in hand, foot, or mind, foursquare... And though it come of a wise man, I hold not with the saying of Pittacus, “Tis hard to be good.’ [i. e. P. does not go far enough for me] Such is the lot of a God alone; as for a man, he cannot but be evil if he be overtaken by hopeless calamity; for any man is good in good fortune and bad in bad, and take it all in all, they are best who are loved by the Gods. [i.e. lucky] Therefore never will I cast my portion of life profitless away upon a hope unaccomplisliable, by going in quest of what cannot be, to wit a man without spot or blemish among all of us who win the fruit of the wide-set earth, but if so be I should come upon him I will send you word of it. My praise and friendship is for all them that of themselves earn no disgrace: even Gods fight not against necessity... I am no faultfinder; enough for me is he that is not good nor yet too exceeding wicked, that knoweth that Right which aideth cities, a sound man. Him will I never blame. For the generation of the worthless is without number, and surely all is fair wherein is mixed nothing foul.<\/span>"}, {"st": "20", "sp": "N", "t": "<span class=\"sm-src\">Himerius Declamations<\/span><span class=\"sm-ctx\">Simonides of Ceos when bidding farewell to Hiero as the king left Sicily for abroad, touched the lyre, but mingled tears with the notes he played.<\/span>"}, {"st": "—", "sp": "HDR", "t": "BOOK VI. DIRGES"}, {"st": "—", "sp": "HDR", "t": "On Those Who Fell at Thermopylae"}, {"st": "21", "sp": "N", "t": "<span class=\"sm-ctx\">Diodorus of Sicily: Historical Library: And for this their valour has been lauded not only by the historians but by many of the poets, and among these the lyrist Simonides, who wrote them a eulogy worthy of their noble deed, in which he says:<\/span>"}, {"st": "—", "sp": "N", "t": "<span class=\"sm-frag\">To them that fell at Thermopylae belong a glorious fortune and a noble lot; for grave they have an altar, for libation-ewers remembrance, and the wine that comes thereof is praise. Such burial neither shall Decay darken, nor Time the all-vanquisher bedim. This shrine of brave men hath taken for its keeper the fair fame of Greece, witness Leonidas the king of Sparta by token of the great ornament of valour and the everlasting glory that he hath left behind.<\/span>"}, {"st": "22", "sp": "N", "t": "<span class=\"sm-src\">Menander On Eulogies<\/span><span class=\"sm-ctx\">Hymns are said to be factitious when we personify abstractions as Gods and children of Gods or of spirits for the occasion, like Simonides when he makes a spirit of To-morrow, and others of Hesitation and what not.<\/span>"}, {"st": "—", "sp": "N", "t": "<span class=\"sm-src\">Stobaeus Anthology [That human prosperity is uncertain because Fortune turns so easily]<\/span><span class=\"sm-ctx\">From the Dirges of Simonides:<\/span>"}, {"st": "—", "sp": "N", "t": "<span class=\"sm-frag\">If thou be’st a mortal man, never say what To-morrow will bring, nor when thou seest a man happy, how long he shall be happy. For swift is change — nay, not so swift the changing course of the wide-wingèd fly. [apparently ‘dragon-fly’]<\/span>"}, {"st": "—", "sp": "HDR", "t": "On the Scopads"}, {"st": "23", "sp": "N", "t": "<span class=\"sm-src\">Scholiast on Theocritus [‘the Scopads’]<\/span><span class=\"sm-ctx\">These were of Crannon, which is a city of Thessaly whence came Scopas son of Creon and Echecrateia. Compare Simonides in the Dirges.<\/span>"}, {"st": "—", "sp": "N", "t": "<span class=\"sm-src\">The Same [‘the Ceian bard’]<\/span><span class=\"sm-ctx\">That is, Simonides, because he wrote Victory-Songs and Dirges for the aforesaid famous Thessalians.<\/span>"}, {"st": "—", "sp": "HDR", "t": "On Antiochus"}, {"st": "24", "sp": "N", "t": "<span class=\"sm-src\">Aristides Orations [The funeral speech on Eteoneus]<\/span><span class=\"sm-ctx\">What Simonides, what Pindar shall bewail such a thing as this? What tune or word shall Stesichorus find worthy of so great a calamity? What Dyseris of Thessaly ever made such lament for the death of an Antiochus, as this mother makes for her son?<\/span>"}, {"st": "—", "sp": "N", "t": "<span class=\"sm-src\">Scholiast on Theocritus [‘many in the house of Antiochus and king Aleuas’]<\/span><span class=\"sm-ctx\">Antiochus was the son of Echecratidas and Dyseris, as we know from Simonides.<\/span>"}, {"st": "—", "sp": "HDR", "t": "On Lysimachus"}, {"st": "25", "sp": "N", "t": "<span class=\"sm-src\">Harpocration Lexicon to the Attic Orators<\/span><span class=\"sm-ctx\">Tamynae: .... a city of the Eretrians in Euboea, containing a temple of Apollo, as we learn from the authors of the Euboica and from Simonides in the Dirge for Lysimachus of Erelria.<\/span>"}, {"st": "26", "sp": "N", "t": "<span class=\"sm-src\">Stobaeus Anthology [That life is short, of little account, and full of care]<\/span><span class=\"sm-ctx\">From the Dirges of Simonides:<\/span>"}, {"st": "—", "sp": "N", "t": "<span class=\"sm-frag\">For even they that were of old time and were born half-immortal sons of most high Gods, came not unto old age without toil, nor without the malice of men, nor without peril.<\/span>"}, {"st": "27", "sp": "N", "t": "<span class=\"sm-src\">Dionysius of Halicarnassus [On verse composition which greatly resembles prose]<\/span><span class=\"sm-ctx\">Take from the lyric poetry this of Simonides, which is here written with the pause-marks corresponding not to the metrical lines arranged by Aristophanes of Byzantium or another, but to the requirements of the words treated as prose. Pray consider the poem and read this part of it according to the pause-marks, and you may be quite sure that the rhythm of the poem will escape you and you will be unable to pick out strophe, antistrophe, or epode; the poem will appear to you mere prose. It is Danaë on the sea, bewailing her fate:<\/span>"}, {"st": "—", "sp": "N", "t": "<span class=\"sm-frag\">When the wind came blowing upon the carven chest and the swaying sea bent her towards fear and tears that would not be stayed from her cheeks, she threw a loving arm round Perseus, saying, ‘O babe, what woe is thine! and yet thou weepest not, but slumberest in thy suckling’s way as thou best night-bound in the black darkness [not of real night, but of the closed chest; the spray came through the air-holes which are represented on a vase-painting (Hermitage 1723, Harrison and MacColl 34) and through which Comatas was fed by the bees (Theocr. 7. 80)] of a dismal brass-ribbed bark, and reckest not of the salt of the passing wave so thick on thy hair, nay, nor the cry of the wind, lying in thy purple swathings with thy pretty face against me. For if the dire were dire to thee, thou’dst lend thy little ear to what I say. So sleep thou on, my baby, as I pray the sea may sleep and our great great woe may sleep; and come some change to us, Father Zeus, of thee. And whatsoever of my prayer be overbold and wrong, do thou forgive it me.’<\/span>"}, {"st": "28", "sp": "N", "t": "<span class=\"sm-src\">Stobaeus Anthology [On death and its inevitability]<\/span><span class=\"sm-ctx\">By Simonides:<\/span><span class=\"sm-q\">For all things come at last to the same horrible Charybdis, great achievements and riches too.<\/span>"}, {"st": "29", "sp": "N", "t": "<span class=\"sm-src\">Plutarch Letter of Consolation to Apollonius<\/span><span class=\"sm-ctx\">For Simonides says:<\/span>"}, {"st": "—", "sp": "N", "t": "<span class=\"sm-frag\">Little is man’s strength and his cares unavailing, and ’tis toil upon toil for him in a life that is short; for all he can do, there’s a death hangs over him that will not be escaped, in which both good men and bad must share alike.<\/span>"}, {"st": "30", "sp": "N", "t": "<span class=\"sm-src\">Athenaeus Doctors at Dinner [on the word γαλαθηνός ‘suckling’]<\/span><span class=\"sm-ctx\">And in another passage Simonides says of Archemorus:<\/span>"}, {"st": "—", "sp": "N", "t": "<span class=\"sm-frag\">They wept as the suckling babe of violet-crowned Eurydice breathed out its sweet soul.<\/span>"}, {"st": "31", "sp": "N", "t": "<span class=\"sm-src\">Diogenes Laertius [on Cleobulus]<\/span><span class=\"sm-ctx\">This man composed poems and riddles to the amount of 3000 lines, and is considered by some writers to be the author of the Epitaph on Midas: ‘A maiden of brass am I, and I lie on the tomb of Midas. So long as water shall flow and tall trees grow green, sun rise and shine and moon give light, rivers run and sea wash shore, ever shall I abide upon this sore-lamented tomb and tell the passers-by that this is the grave of Midas.’ And they find evidence for this in a poem of Simonides, where he says:<\/span>"}, {"st": "—", "sp": "N", "t": "<span class=\"sm-frag\">Who that hath understanding would praise Cleobulus the man of Lindus for his pitting of the might of a gravestone against the ever-running rivers and the flowers of the Spring, against the flame of sun and of golden moon, and against the eddies of the ocean-wave? All these are subject to the Gods; but a stone, even mortal hands may break it. This is the rede of a fool.<\/span>"}, {"st": "32", "sp": "N", "t": "<span class=\"sm-src\">Theophilus of Antioch To Autolycus [on Providence]<\/span><span class=\"sm-ctx\">Simonides:<\/span>"}, {"st": "—", "sp": "N", "t": "<span class=\"sm-frag\">None getteth achievement without the Gods, neither man nor city. He that can devise all is a God, and there’s nothing to be got among men without toil.<\/span>"}, {"st": "33", "sp": "N", "t": "<span class=\"sm-src\">The Same [that the judgment of God will come, and misfortune will take the wicked unawares]<\/span><span class=\"sm-ctx\">Simonides:<\/span>"}, {"st": "—", "sp": "N", "t": "<span class=\"sm-frag\">There’s no ill that a man must not expect, and ’tis not long ere God turneth all things upside-down.<\/span>"}, {"st": "34", "sp": "N", "t": "<span class=\"sm-src\">Plutarch Should Old Men Govern?<\/span><span class=\"sm-ctx\">From a lawful and democratic constitution the death of a man who has always suffered himself to be ruled for his advantage no less than to rule, receives in the fame his life has won, to quote Simonides, indeed<\/span>"}, {"st": "—", "sp": "N", "t": "<span class=\"sm-frag\">a fair funeral-offering; for such sinketh last of all offerings into the ground.<\/span>"}, {"st": "35", "sp": "N", "t": "<span class=\"sm-src\">Scholiast on Sophocles [‘on things full done’]<\/span><span class=\"sm-ctx\">Upon that which is done and cannot be cured; compare Simonides:<\/span>"}, {"st": "—", "sp": "N", "t": "<span class=\"sm-frag\">For what once hath happened cannot be undone.<\/span>"}, {"st": "—", "sp": "HDR", "t": "BOOK VII. VICTORY-SONGS FOR RUNNERS"}, {"st": "36", "sp": "N", "t": "<span class=\"sm-src\">Photius Lexicon<\/span><span class=\"sm-ctx\">περιαγειρομενοι ‘going round collecting’: ... after this it became the custom for the athletes to walk round and ‘collect’ or receive what was given them; whence Simonides says of Astylus:<\/span>"}, {"st": "—", "sp": "N", "t": "<span class=\"sm-frag\">Who among those of our time ever bound upon him so many victories with leaves of myrtle or wreaths of roses in a contest of the men of those parts?<\/span>"}, {"st": "—", "sp": "HDR", "t": "BOOK VIII. VICTORY-SONGS FOR FIVE-EVENT-CHAMPIONS"}, {"st": "37", "sp": "N", "t": "<span class=\"sm-src\">Aristotle History of Animals<\/span><span class=\"sm-ctx\">The halcyon nests about the time of the winter-solstice, and that is why, when the weather is fine at that time of year we call the days ‘halcyon-days,’ being seven before and seven after the shortest day of the year; compare Simonides:<\/span>"}, {"st": "—", "sp": "N", "t": "<span class=\"sm-frag\">As when in the month of winter Zeus monisheth [lit. ‘teaches to behave correctly, brings to a proper frame of mind’] the fourteen days, and mankind call it the sacred windless season when the pied halcyon rears her young.<\/span>"}, {"st": "—", "sp": "HDR", "t": "BOOK IX. VICTORY-SONGS FOR WRESTLERS"}, {"st": "38", "sp": "N", "t": "<span class=\"sm-src\">Scholiasts on Aristophanes [‘First I took the lyre and bade him sing a song of Simonides about the shearing of the Ram’]<\/span><span class=\"sm-ctx\">The beginning of a song to (?) Crins (Ram) of Aegina [cf. Hdt. 6. 73, 85, 6. 50 — ]... It is (or he is) apparently famous. — This poem comes from a Song of Victory of Simonides... Crius was an Aeginetan wrestler... the poet says:<\/span>"}, {"st": "—", "sp": "N", "t": "<span class=\"sm-frag\">Master Ram yielded no scanty fleece when he came to the fine woody precinct of Zeus.<\/span>"}, {"st": "—", "sp": "HDR", "t": "BOOK X. VICTORY-SONGS FOR BOXERS"}, {"st": "—", "sp": "HDR", "t": "For Glaucus of Carystus"}, {"st": "39", "sp": "N", "t": "<span class=\"sm-src\">Lucian On Behalf of the Pictures<\/span><span class=\"sm-ctx\">But think how a famous poet praised Glaucus saying:<\/span>"}, {"st": "—", "sp": "N", "t": "<span class=\"sm-frag\">Neither the might of Polydeuces would have lift hand against him, nay, nor the iron child of Alcmena. [Heracles]<\/span>"}, {"st": "—", "sp": "N", "t": "<span class=\"sm-ctx\">Do you see to what Gods he likened him, or rather declared him to surpass? And neither was Glaucus himself offended at being praised at the expense of the Gods who are guardians of athletes, nor did those Gods punish either Glaucus or the poet for impiety. Far from it, both of them received honour and glory from all Greece, the one for his strength and the other for no poem that he wrote more than for this. [Glaucus won at Olympia in 480]<\/span>"}, {"st": "—", "sp": "HDR", "t": "BOOK XI. VICTORY-SONGS FOR THE FOUR-HORSE-CHARIOT-RACE"}, {"st": "40", "sp": "N", "t": "<span class=\"sm-note\">[cf. Eust. Op. 279]<\/span>"}, {"st": "—", "sp": "N", "t": "<span class=\"sm-src\">Aristophanes Knights<\/span><span class=\"sm-ctx\">[CHORUS to CLEON]: O how I wish you might throw up your mouthful [free dinners at the Town-hall] as readily as you found it. Then, it ever, should I sing<\/span>"}, {"st": "—", "sp": "N", "t": "<span class=\"sm-frag\">Drink, O drink when things go right;<\/span>"}, {"st": "—", "sp": "N", "t": "<span class=\"sm-frag\">and I believe that the man from lulls, being an old chap with an eye for the — loaves, [the Greek is ‘ogler of loaves’ for ‘ogler of lads’] would have sung his Hail-to-Apollo’s and Great-God-of-Wine’s with a right good will for the occasion.<\/span>"}, {"st": "—", "sp": "N", "t": "<span class=\"sm-src\">Scholiast on the passage<\/span><span class=\"sm-ctx\">He means: Then I should sing you Simonides’ song ‘Drink’ etc.: the song comes from Simonides’ Four-Horse-Chariot-Race Songs, and the word συμφορά [which usually means ‘misfortune’] is here used of good luck. For it is really colourless [meaning an event].<\/span>"}, {"st": "—", "sp": "HDR", "t": "For Xenocrates of Agrigentum"}, {"st": "41", "sp": "N", "t": "<span class=\"sm-note\">[cf. Pind. P. 6]<\/span>"}, {"st": "—", "sp": "N", "t": "<span class=\"sm-src\">Scholiast on Pindar Introduction to Pythian I<\/span><span class=\"sm-ctx\">This Xenocrates won in the horse-race not only at the Isthmian Festival, but at the Pythian in the 24th Pythiad (B.C. 490) according to Aristotle’s list. Simonides includes both the victories in his celebration of the victor.<\/span>"}, {"st": "—", "sp": "HDR", "t": "For Orilias"}, {"st": "42", "sp": "N", "t": "<span class=\"sm-src\">Diogenian Preface to Proverbs<\/span><span class=\"sm-ctx\">A ‘Carian tale’ is one which is told of a Carian fisherman who said when he saw an octopus one winter’s day ‘If I strip and dive for him I shall catch cold, and if I don’t take him my children will die of hunger.’ Timocreon uses this story in his Lyric Poems, and Simonides refers to it in his Epinician Ode for Orillas.<\/span>"}, {"st": "—", "sp": "N", "t": "<span class=\"sm-src\">Proverbs (Cohn)<\/span><span class=\"sm-ctx\">‘The Carian tale’: — mentioned by Simonides in his praises of a charioteer who won at Pellenè and received for his prize a cloak with which he kept off the cold ⟨for the.... games were held⟩ at Pellenè in winter. They say that a fisherman said on seeing an octopus in the winter ‘If I don’t dive I shall starve,’ and that this is the Carian tale.<\/span>"}, {"st": "—", "sp": "N", "t": "<span class=\"sm-src\">Athenaeus Doctors at Dinner [on octopuses]<\/span><span class=\"sm-ctx\">The Dorians, for instance Epicharmus, give it the ω, πώλυπος ‘octopus,’ and Simonides says:<\/span>"}, {"st": "—", "sp": "N", "t": "<span class=\"sm-frag\">searching for an octopus<\/span>"}, {"st": "—", "sp": "HDR", "t": "For Scopas"}, {"st": "43", "sp": "N", "t": "<span class=\"sm-src\">Cicero On the Orator<\/span><span class=\"sm-ctx\">I am grateful to Simonides of Ceos for his invention — if his it was — of the art of mnemonics. For there is a story that one day when Simonides was dining at Crannon in Thessaly with a wealthy noble named Scopas, and sang a song which he had written in his honour and which contained by way of poetic ornament much praise of Castor and Pollux, Scopas ungenerously remarked that he should give the poet only half of the price agreed for it; be must please to go for the rest to the precious deities who had received half his praise. Shortly afterwards, having received a message that two young men wanted him urgently outside, Simonides rose from the table and went to the door, only to find nobody there. That very moment Scopas’ dining-chamber collapsed, and he and his perished in the ruins. Now when their kinsfolk wished to bury them they found it was impossible to identify the remains. But we are told that Simonides was able from his recollection of the place each guest occupied at table to do so for them in every case. This it was, they say, which led to his discovery that the chief aid to memory is arrangement.<\/span>"}, {"st": "—", "sp": "HDR", "t": "For Eualcides"}, {"st": "44", "sp": "N", "t": "<span class=\"sm-src\">Herodotus History<\/span><span class=\"sm-ctx\">(see p. 247)<\/span>"}, {"st": "45", "sp": "N", "t": "<span class=\"sm-src\">Plutarch How to distinguish a Friend from a Flatterer<\/span><span class=\"sm-ctx\">And moreover, as Simonides says:The rearing of horses goeth not with Zacynthus, but with fields that bear wheat.<\/span>"}, {"st": "—", "sp": "N", "t": "<span class=\"sm-frag\">Thus we see flattery not following after the poor or obscure or weak, but becoming a pitfall and a plague to great houses and mighty undertakings.<\/span>"}, {"st": "—", "sp": "HDR", "t": "BOOK XII. VICTORY-SONGS FOR THE MULE-CAR-RACE"}, {"st": "—", "sp": "HDR", "t": "For Anaxilas of Rhegium"}, {"st": "46", "sp": "N", "t": "<span class=\"sm-note\">[μεταμάνιos ‘to the winds’ is prob. connected with Aeol. for άνεμος ‘wind’]<\/span>"}, {"st": "—", "sp": "N", "t": "<span class=\"sm-src\">Aristotle Rhetoric<\/span><span class=\"sm-ctx\">Once when Simonides was offered too small a fee by the victor in the mule-race, he refused to write him an ode, on the plea that he could not bring himself to write in honour of mules. But when he offered him sufficient pay, he took it and wrote:<\/span>"}, {"st": "—", "sp": "N", "t": "<span class=\"sm-q\">Hail, ye daughters of storm-footed steeds!<\/span><span class=\"sm-ctx\">And yet they were also daughters of asses.<\/span>"}, {"st": "47", "sp": "N", "t": "<span class=\"sm-ctx\">Scholiast on Aristophanes Peace [‘... comes the report that thou wilt leave me to the birds and go to the deuce and the winds?’]: — The word μεταμώνιος is taken by some authorities to mean ‘vain and useless’; others say it means properly ‘that which goes aloft on both sides,’ quoting in support of this Simonides:<\/span>"}, {"st": "—", "sp": "N", "t": "<span class=\"sm-frag\">And the dust from the wheel went up to the winds.<\/span>"}, {"st": "48", "sp": "N", "t": "<span class=\"sm-src\">Plutarch Moral Virtue<\/span><span class=\"sm-ctx\">... just as Plato [Phaedr. 254] employs the simile of the draught-horses of the soul, the worser horse struggling against the better in the shafts, and disquieting the driver, who has for ever to be carefully holding them and tightening the rein,<\/span>"}, {"st": "—", "sp": "N", "t": "<span class=\"sm-ctx\"><i class=\"sm-qi\">lest he lose his hold on the crimson thongs<\/i> in Simonides’ phrase.<\/span>"}, {"st": "49", "sp": "N", "t": "<span class=\"sm-src\">Athenaeus Doctors at Dinner [on the Pleiads]<\/span><span class=\"sm-ctx\">— Simonides calls them Peleiades in the following passage:<\/span>"}, {"st": "—", "sp": "N", "t": "<span class=\"sm-frag\">Now he that gives it thee [the prize to the winner] is Hermes God of the Games, Son of mountain Maia of the glancing eye, who was the fairest of all Atlas’ seven violet-tressèd daughters dear, that are called the Heavenly Peleiades.<\/span>"}, {"st": "50", "sp": "N", "t": "<span class=\"sm-src\">Scholiast on Pindar [‘of Pisa’]<\/span><span class=\"sm-ctx\">— The first syllable of Πισας must be made short to preserve the antistrophic correspondence; both Pindar and Simonides do this.<\/span>"}, {"st": "51", "sp": "N", "t": "<span class=\"sm-ctx\">[here follow the unplaceable lyric fragments]Tzetzes Chiliads: ... as Simonides writes of Orpheus:<\/span>"}, {"st": "—", "sp": "N", "t": "<span class=\"sm-frag\">Above his head there hovered birds innumerable, and fishes leapt clean from the blue water because of his sweet music.<\/span>"}, {"st": "52", "sp": "N", "t": "<span class=\"sm-src\">Plutarch Dinner-Table Problems [Why sounds are clearer at night than by day]<\/span><span class=\"sm-ctx\">— For a calm or absence of wind is favourable to sound, and the opposite unfavourable, as Simonides says:<\/span>"}, {"st": "—", "sp": "N", "t": "<span class=\"sm-frag\">For then there was wind not so much as the breath that maketh leaves quiver, to stay the honey-sweet voice from its goal in the ears of man.<\/span>"}, {"st": "53", "sp": "N", "t": "<span class=\"sm-src\">Stobaeus Selections [on those who explain divine things, and that the real truth of abstract conceptions is unattainable]<\/span><span class=\"sm-ctx\">— Simonides:<\/span>"}, {"st": "—", "sp": "N", "t": "<span class=\"sm-frag\">’Tis easy for a God to steal the wits of a man.<\/span>"}, {"st": "54", "sp": "N", "t": "<span class=\"sm-src\">Scholiast on Apollonius of Rhodes Argonautica<\/span><span class=\"sm-ctx\">Apollonius makes Love the son of Aphrodite... Simonides of Aphrodite and Ares:<\/span>"}, {"st": "—", "sp": "N", "t": "<span class=\"sm-frag\">Thou cruel child of wile-contriving Aphrodite, whom she bore to wile-devising Ares.<\/span>"}, {"st": "55,56", "sp": "N", "t": "<span class=\"sm-src\">Plutarch Why the Oracles are no longer in Metre<\/span><span class=\"sm-ctx\">— So we went round and sat down on the southern basement of the temple, [of Apollo at Delphi] looking at the chapel of Earth and the water there. Whereupon Boëthus exclaimed that the place contributed to the stranger’s bewilderment. For there was a chapel of the Muses there, where the spring rises, which is why they used this water for libations; compare Simonides:<\/span>"}, {"st": "—", "sp": "N", "t": "<span class=\"sm-q\">... where they draw the pure lustration-water from beneath the place of the fair-tressed Muses.<\/span><span class=\"sm-ctx\">And again Simonides addressing Clio says a little more elaborately:<\/span>"}, {"st": "—", "sp": "N", "t": "<span class=\"sm-frag\">Thou overseer of the pure lustration-water, receiver of the prayers of many a pitcher-carrier, who givest free course through a golden pipe to the lovely liquor that comes of the ambrosial cave; [cf. Poulsen Delphi 4]<\/span>"}, {"st": "—", "sp": "N", "t": "<span class=\"sm-ctx\">Eudoxus, therefore, is wrong in agreeing with the writers who make out that it is the water of the Styx that is so called.<\/span>"}, {"st": "57", "sp": "N", "t": "<span class=\"sm-src\">Plutarch How a Man may perceive that he is progressing in Virtue<\/span><span class=\"sm-ctx\">Just as Simonides says thatThe bee consorteth with the flowers to contrive her yellow honey,<\/span>"}, {"st": "—", "sp": "N", "t": "<span class=\"sm-frag\">and neither likes nor takes anything else from them, whereas others like their colour and scent, so the man who, unlike those who have recourse to poetry for pleasure and amusement, finds for himself in it and adds to his store some treasure that is really worth having — such a man may be reckoned to have won by mere familiarity a power to appreciate what is beautiful and proper.<\/span>"}, {"st": "58", "sp": "N", "t": "<span class=\"sm-src\">Scholiast on the Iliad [on round numbers]<\/span><span class=\"sm-ctx\">... For Homer says ‘Nineteen were there of my one womb’ [Il. 24. 496], and Simonides:<\/span>"}, {"st": "—", "sp": "N", "t": "<span class=\"sm-frag\">And gracious be thou, O Mother of twenty children.<\/span>"}, {"st": "59", "sp": "N", "t": "<span class=\"sm-note\">[cf. Plut. Vit. Dion. 1, Arist. Il h. 1. 6, Cram. A.P. 1. 285. 8]<\/span>"}, {"st": "—", "sp": "N", "t": "<span class=\"sm-ctx\">Scholiast on Pindar [‘how once before the walls of Dardanus the Corinthians were thought to be turning the issues of fights either way’]: — The Corinthians distinguished themselves on both sides at Troy, fighting both with the Trojans and with the Greeks... And this is why Simonides says:<\/span>"}, {"st": "—", "sp": "N", "t": "<span class=\"sm-frag\">Troy is not wroth with the men of Corinth, Greeks though they be;<br>for they were allies of both sides.<\/span>"}, {"st": "60", "sp": "N", "t": "<span class=\"sm-src\">Plutarch Exile<\/span><span class=\"sm-ctx\">If a man will but bear this in mind and keep his head, he will prefer even to live in exile on an island such as Gyaros or Cinaros... without losing heart or lamenting or saying like the women in Simonides<\/span>"}, {"st": "—", "sp": "N", "t": "<span class=\"sm-frag\">The noise of the purple sea-waves about me holds me fast.<\/span>"}, {"st": "61", "sp": "N", "t": "<span class=\"sm-src\">Athenaeus Doctors at Dinner [on the Funeral Games of Pelias]<\/span><span class=\"sm-ctx\">Sound evidence that this poem is the work of Stesichorus is given by the poet Simonides, who in a passage about Meleager says:<\/span>"}, {"st": "—", "sp": "N", "t": "<span class=\"sm-ctx\">who beat all the youth of Iolcos of the vineyards in throwing the spear across the eddies of Anaurus; for thus have Homer and Stesichorus sung to the peoples.<\/span>"}, {"st": "62-64", "sp": "N", "t": "<span class=\"sm-src\">Plutarch Life of Theseus<\/span><span class=\"sm-ctx\">And then, while Theseus cheered his father and boasted how he would vanquish the Minotaur, Aegeus gave the ship’s captain a spare white sail, bidding him hoist the white if he came home with Theseus saved alive, and if not, to sail with the black in token of what had happened. But according to Simonides the sail given by Aegeus was not white, but<\/span>"}, {"st": "—", "sp": "N", "t": "<span class=\"sm-q\">a crimson sail dyed with the flower of the springing holm-oak<br>and Aegeus made this their token that all was well. The captain of the ship was<br>Phereclus son of Amarsyas<\/span><span class=\"sm-ctx\">according to Simonides.<\/span>"}, {"st": "—", "sp": "N", "t": "<span class=\"sm-src\">Scholiast on Sophocles [‘What is it you have left undone?’]<\/span><span class=\"sm-ctx\">that is, what is wanting or lacking to you for this your need? ‘what is lacking’ means that it would have been better for him to come a moment sooner; and in Simonides, of the messenger sent to Aegeus:<\/span>"}, {"st": "—", "sp": "N", "t": "<span class=\"sm-frag\">I would have given thee reward more worth than life itself, hadst thou got hither sooner.<\/span>"}, {"st": "65", "sp": "N", "t": "<span class=\"sm-src\">Clement of Alexandria<\/span><span class=\"sm-ctx\">Cod preaches to us and Him we must believe:— ‘For with the heart man believeth unto righteousness, and with the mouth confession is made unto salvation. For the scripture saith “Whosoever believeth on him shall not be put to shame.”’ (Rom. 10. 10). Well may Simonides write:<\/span>"}, {"st": "—", "sp": "N", "t": "<span class=\"sm-frag\">There’s a tale that Virtue [the Greek word sometimes connotes achievement and the fame it brings] dwelleth on a rook hard to climb and with a pure band of Goddesses to watch over it, nor may she ever be seen by eye of mortal, unless heart-devouring sweat come out of one and he reach unto the very top of manliness.<\/span>"}, {"st": "66", "sp": "N", "t": "<span class=\"sm-src\">Herodian Words without Parallel, πΰρ, ‘fire’<\/span><span class=\"sm-ctx\">— There is no neuter monosyllable in -υρ except this, and Simonides for metre’s sake makes it disyllabic:<\/span>"}, {"st": "—", "sp": "N", "t": "<span class=\"sm-frag\">For fire is what the beasts hate most of all.<\/span>"}, {"st": "67", "sp": "N", "t": "<span class=\"sm-src\">Plutarch How to distinguish, a Friend from a Flatterer<\/span><span class=\"sm-ctx\">The false, counterfeit, base-minted friend... stands in awe of the better man, not ‘going afoot beside a Lydian chariot,’ but in Simonides’ phrase:<\/span>"}, {"st": "—", "sp": "N", "t": "<span class=\"sm-frag\">With pure refinèd gold to his hand, possessing not lead.<\/span>"}, {"st": "68", "sp": "N", "t": "<span class=\"sm-src\">Stobaeus Anthology [on Death and its inevitability]<\/span><span class=\"sm-ctx\">Simonides:<\/span><span class=\"sm-q\">But Death surely overtaketh him that runneth from the battle.<\/span>"}, {"st": "69", "sp": "N", "t": "<span class=\"sm-src\">Plutarch Sayings of Emperors [Augustus Caesar]<\/span><span class=\"sm-ctx\">When the philosopher Athenodorus asked to be allowed to return home because of his age, Augustus agreed; but when the old man on bidding him farewell added ‘When you are angry, Caesar, say nothing and do nothing till you have mentally repeated the alphabet,’ he took him by the hand saying, ‘I still have need of your presence’; and kept him a whole twelvemonth, adding<\/span>"}, {"st": "—", "sp": "N", "t": "<span class=\"sm-frag\">In silence also there’s a worth that brings no risk.<\/span>"}, {"st": "70", "sp": "N", "t": "<span class=\"sm-src\">Sextus Empiricus Against the Mathematicians<\/span><span class=\"sm-ctx\">Health has been declared not only a good, but the first good, by many poets and prose-writers, indeed by all who write of the realities of life; Simonides the lyrist says:<\/span>"}, {"st": "—", "sp": "N", "t": "<span class=\"sm-frag\">There’s no joy even in beautiful Wisdom, unless one have holy Health.<\/span>"}, {"st": "71", "sp": "N", "t": "<span class=\"sm-src\">Athenaeus Doctors at Dinner [on luxury]<\/span><span class=\"sm-ctx\">And pleasure is considered a very great good by the keenest-witted of men, men with the highest reputation for wisdom, for instance Simonides, who says:<\/span>"}, {"st": "—", "sp": "N", "t": "<span class=\"sm-frag\">For what human life, nay, what throne; is desirable without pleasure? Without her the life of a very God is not to be envied.<\/span>"}, {"st": "72", "sp": "N", "t": "<span class=\"sm-src\">Athenaeus Doctors at Dinner [on love]<\/span><span class=\"sm-ctx\">... Sophocles (according to Ion) said to the guest sitting next him, ‘What a pretty phrase that is of Phrynichus: “The light of love shines upon crimson cheeks”!’ Whereupon the Eretrian or Erythraean — and he was an elementary schoolmaster — exclaimed, ‘You may be very clever, Sophocles, at poetry; but all the same, Phrynichus was wrong in calling the cheeks of the pretty one crimson. If the painter were to put crimson on this lad’s cheeks, he would no longer be pretty. And therefore what is pretty ought not to be likened to what is not so.’ To which Sophocles replied with a smile at the Eretrian, Then, sir, this of Simonides, highly approved by our countrymen in general, will likewise meet your censure:<\/span>"}, {"st": "—", "sp": "N", "t": "<span class=\"sm-frag\">The maid sent forth speech from her crimson lips.<\/span>"}, {"st": "73", "sp": "N", "t": "<span class=\"sm-note\">[cf. Sch. Od. 19. 518, Eust. 1875. 41]<\/span>"}, {"st": "—", "sp": "N", "t": "<span class=\"sm-src\">Etymologicum Magnum<\/span><span class=\"sm-ctx\">Green-hued nightingale (Od. 19, 518): — from the colour... Compare Simonides:<\/span>"}, {"st": "—", "sp": "N", "t": "<span class=\"sm-frag\">When the babbling nightingales, the green-necked birds of the Spring<\/span>"}, {"st": "74", "sp": "N", "t": "<span class=\"sm-src\">Scholiast on Aristophanes Birds [‘What birds are these’ etc.]<\/span><span class=\"sm-ctx\">Some say this comes from Alcaeus (fr. 141) and from Simonides:<\/span>"}, {"st": "—", "sp": "N", "t": "<span class=\"sm-ctx\">Loud messenger of sweet-scented Spring, blue Swallow [loud: in the eaves of a morning, cf. Anacreont. 10]<\/span>"}, {"st": "75", "sp": "N", "t": "<span class=\"sm-src\">Scholiast on Pindar [‘praise thou old wine, but the flowers of new songs’]<\/span><span class=\"sm-ctx\">... This appears to be directed against Simonides, who when beaten by Pindar in the contest, wrote abuse of the judge for condemning a good poem. And it is because in this he said:<\/span>"}, {"st": "—", "sp": "N", "t": "<span class=\"sm-frag\">New wine can be known for this year’s gift of the vine, [lit. ‘not yet last year’s’; i. e. it is as easy to tell young work as new wine, and neither is good] and this tale is the work of a fool and a lad,<\/span>"}, {"st": "—", "sp": "N", "t": "<span class=\"sm-frag\">that Pindar here praises old wine.<\/span>"}, {"st": "76", "sp": "N", "t": "<span class=\"sm-src\">Scholiast on Euripides [‘appearance winneth even if it be beside the truth’]<\/span><span class=\"sm-ctx\">Compare Simonides:<\/span>"}, {"st": "—", "sp": "N", "t": "<span class=\"sm-frag\">Appearance forceth even the truth.<\/span>"}, {"st": "77", "sp": "N", "t": "<span class=\"sm-src\">Theodorus the Metochite Prelude<\/span><span class=\"sm-ctx\">In the words of Simonides, <i class=\"sm-qi\">The sky hath nought but the sun;<\/i><\/span>"}, {"st": "—", "sp": "N", "t": "<span class=\"sm-frag\">and in like manner those who concern themselves with philosophy have nought but Aristotle and his writings for a complete exposition of it.<\/span>"}, {"st": "78", "sp": "N", "t": "<span class=\"sm-src\">Scholiast on the Iliad [‘A fish leaping in the wave shall dart beneath the dark ripple’]<\/span><span class=\"sm-ctx\">φρίξ, ‘a ripple,’ is the beginning of a rising wind. Simonides tries to indicate it thus:<\/span>"}, {"st": "—", "sp": "N", "t": "<span class=\"sm-frag\">A breeze comes stippling the sea.<\/span>"}, {"st": "79", "sp": "N", "t": "<span class=\"sm-src\">The Same [‘sweet sleep’]<\/span><span class=\"sm-ctx\">... Poets after Homer also use the form without the ν, ήδυμος... Compare Simonides:<\/span>"}, {"st": "—", "sp": "N", "t": "<span class=\"sm-frag\">but he, possessing sweet slumber<\/span>"}, {"st": "80", "sp": "N", "t": "<span class=\"sm-src\">Cramer Inedita (Paris)<\/span><span class=\"sm-ctx\">νίκη ‘victory’: — Apollonius son of Archibius says that it stands for ο εν εικει, that is ‘that which yields to one,’ being formed by the dropping of the initial e and syncope of the diphthong et. [the etymology is hardly correct] Simonides alludes to the etymology of it in the lines:<\/span>"}, {"st": "—", "sp": "N", "t": "<span class=\"sm-frag\">To one alone did the Goddess yield when she mounted the chariot.<\/span>"}, {"st": "81", "sp": "N", "t": "<span class=\"sm-src\">Athenaeus Doctors at Dinner [on poultry]<\/span><span class=\"sm-ctx\">The forms άλεκτορίς ‘hen’ and αλέκτωρ ‘cock’ also occur: compare Simonides:<\/span>"}, {"st": "—", "sp": "N", "t": "<span class=\"sm-frag\">Deliglitsome-voicèd chanticleer<\/span>"}, {"st": "82", "sp": "N", "t": "<span class=\"sm-src\">Scholiast on the Iliad [‘a wave reared by the wind’]<\/span><span class=\"sm-ctx\">Simonides speaks of <i class=\"sm-qi\">gates reared by the wind<\/i><\/span>"}, {"st": "83", "sp": "N", "t": "<span class=\"sm-src\">The Same [‘all-subduing sleep’]<\/span><span class=\"sm-ctx\">πανδαμάτωp means ‘that which leaves no man unsubdued’: Simonides calls sleep<\/span>"}, {"st": "—", "sp": "N", "t": "<span class=\"sm-frag\">man-subduing<\/span>"}, {"st": "84", "sp": "N", "t": "<span class=\"sm-src\">Choeroboscus on Theodosius<\/span><span class=\"sm-ctx\">τρνγλωχιν ‘three-barbed’... the form ending in v rather than s, as in Simonides<\/span>"}, {"st": "—", "sp": "N", "t": "<span class=\"sm-frag\">three-barbèd arrow<br>is rarely found.<\/span>"}, {"st": "85", "sp": "N", "t": "<span class=\"sm-src\">Scholiast on Theocritus [‘This is Thyrsis of Etna’]<\/span><span class=\"sm-ctx\">Etna is a mountain in Sicily... According to Simonides, Etna decided between Hephaestus and Demeter when they quarrelled over the possession of the country.<\/span>"}, {"st": "86,87,88", "sp": "N", "t": "<span class=\"sm-note\">[cf. Ath. 5. 181b, Eust. 1166. 49: these three fragments are now generally ascribed to Pindar, prob. rightly]<\/span>"}, {"st": "—", "sp": "N", "t": "<span class=\"sm-src\">Plutarch Dinner-Table Problems [What features Poetry and Dancing have in common]<\/span><span class=\"sm-ctx\">In short the saying of Simonides must be changed, he said, from painting to dancing, and we must say (not that painting but) that the dance is a silent poetry and poetry a speaking dance... And it would appear that, as if it were a matter of painting, the poems themselves are like the colours, and the dances to which they belong like the outlines which the colours fill. And the poet who is thought to have done his best and most expressive work in the Hyporcheme or Dance-Song proves that the two arts (of dancing and poetry) stand in need of one another; compare:<\/span>"}, {"st": "—", "sp": "N", "t": "<span class=\"sm-frag\">Come pursue the curving course of the tune, and imitate with foot a-whirl in the contest unapproachable horse or Amyclean hound;<\/span>"}, {"st": "—", "sp": "N", "t": "<span class=\"sm-ctx\">or this:<\/span>"}, {"st": "—", "sp": "N", "t": "<span class=\"sm-frag\">And even as on the windy Dotian plain a hound doth fly to find death for a horned hind, and she turns the head upon her neck this, that, and every way<\/span>"}, {"st": "—", "sp": "N", "t": "<span class=\"sm-ctx\">and the rest: — these passages may almost be said to call down the subject-matter of the dance from heaven above, and to pull and guide one’s hands and feet, or rather one’s whole body, with the puppet-strings of its music, the body being unable to keep still while they are being sung or said. At any rate he takes no shame to himself to praise his own dance any more than his own poetry; compare:<\/span>"}, {"st": "—", "sp": "N", "t": "<span class=\"sm-frag\">And when I shall sing the bride, I know well how to mingle the light dance of the feet. The style of it is called the Cretan, and the instrument of music is Molossian.<\/span>"}, {"st": "—", "sp": "HDR", "t": "BOOK XIII. ELEGIACS"}, {"st": "—", "sp": "HDR", "t": "On the Battle of Marathon"}, {"st": "89-90", "sp": "N", "t": "<span class=\"sm-note\">[cf. Vit. Aesch. Blog. Gr. 119 ‘According to some authorities Aeschylus was defeated by Simonides in the Elegy on Those who fell at Marathon’]<\/span>"}, {"st": "—", "sp": "N", "t": "<span class=\"sm-ctx\">Scholiast on Aristophanes [‘Now if it is right, thou Daughter of Zeus, to honour him that is the best and most famous author of comedy in the world, great praise is due, says our poet, tome’]: This comes from Simonides’ Elegiacs:<\/span>"}, {"st": "—", "sp": "N", "t": "<span class=\"sm-frag\">But if it is right, thou Daughter of Zeus, [Truth] to honour him that is best, it was none but the people of Athens, though I say it, that did this thing.<\/span>"}, {"st": "90", "sp": "N", "t": "<span class=\"sm-src\">Scholiast on Gregory of Nazianzen<\/span><span class=\"sm-ctx\">He means that whereas guiltlessness is superhuman, to repair a small error marks the good man and true. Simonides, one of the Nine Lyric Poets, in an epigram [or inscription] he wrote on the Athenians who fell at Marathon has the following line:<\/span>"}, {"st": "—", "sp": "N", "t": "<span class=\"sm-ctx\">To incur no guilt and accomplish all things is the mark of a God. [the ascription can hardly be correct, as the line occurs in the Chaeronea-epitaph Dem. Crown 288]<\/span>"}, {"st": "—", "sp": "HDR", "t": "On the Sea-fight at Salamis"}, {"st": "91", "sp": "N", "t": "<span class=\"sm-src\">Plutarch Themistocles<\/span><span class=\"sm-ctx\">— The rest, their inferiority in numbers being compensated by the narrowness of the strait, which both prevented the Barbarians from using their whole force at once and caused their ships to fall foul of one another,<\/span>"}, {"st": "—", "sp": "N", "t": "<span class=\"sm-frag\">held out till fall of night<\/span>"}, {"st": "—", "sp": "N", "t": "<span class=\"sm-ctx\">as Simonides says, and thus won a great and famous victory which outshone any deed of the sea whether Greek or Barbarian, alike in the fire and courage of the men and in the genius of their leader.<\/span>"}, {"st": "—", "sp": "HDR", "t": "On the Battle of Plataea"}, {"st": "92", "sp": "N", "t": "<span class=\"sm-src\">Plutarch The Malignity of Herodotus<\/span><span class=\"sm-ctx\">But of the Corinthians and their position in the battle, and what they gained from the conflict at Plataea, we may learn from Simonides, who writes:<\/span>"}, {"st": "—", "sp": "N", "t": "<span class=\"sm-ctx\">Midmost stood the dwellers in Ephyra of the many fountains, men versed in every virtue of war:<\/span><span class=\"sm-ctx\">and this:<\/span>"}, {"st": "—", "sp": "N", "t": "<span class=\"sm-frag\">And those that live in Corinth town, the city of Glaucus, made unto themselves a right noble witness of their deeds by honouring the gold that is in the sky; [the sun] and that gold doth increase and spread wide their fame and the fame of their fathers; for whereas gold is the kindest of all hosts when it shineth in the sky, [it comes an evil guest unto those that receive it in their hand]. [they had refused the Persian bribes, cf. 117. 2]<\/span>"}, {"st": "—", "sp": "N", "t": "<span class=\"sm-frag\">He has not said this in any work for which he trained a chorus at Corinth nor in any poem written in honour of that city, but has simply recorded their deeds in an elegiac poem.<\/span>"}, {"st": "93", "sp": "N", "t": "<span class=\"sm-ctx\">Ammianus Marcellinus History. But the magnificence of these assemblies is spoilt by the uncouth irresponsibility of a few who forget their station in life, and through being allowed licence slip into licentiousness. As the lyrist Simonides says:<\/span>"}, {"st": "—", "sp": "N", "t": "<span class=\"sm-frag\">For he that would live completely happy must before all things belong to a country that is of fair report.<\/span>"}, {"st": "94", "sp": "N", "t": "<span class=\"sm-ctx\">Claudian Letters [to Probinus]:<\/span><span class=\"sm-q\">Fortune helps the brave<\/span>"}, {"st": "—", "sp": "N", "t": "<span class=\"sm-ctx\">is the maxim of the poet of Ceos; and whither it leads, though you were silent, I should not hesitate to go.<\/span>"}, {"st": "95", "sp": "N", "t": "<span class=\"sm-src\">Plutarch Should Old Men Govern?<\/span><span class=\"sm-ctx\">Simonides’ dictum<\/span><span class=\"sm-q\">The city is the teacher of the man<br>applies to those who have still time to be taught better and mend their ways.<\/span>"}, {"st": "96", "sp": "N", "t": "<span class=\"sm-src\">Cramer Inedita (Paris)<\/span><span class=\"sm-ctx\">Pray listen to what Crobylus said. He was an Athenian who advised his fellow-countrymen to turn a deaf ear to Philip of Macedon’s proposals for peace. ‘...if, that is, you will cease vain talk, if you will free Greece and regain your traditional hegemony, both of which are now, in the words of Simonides,<\/span>"}, {"st": "—", "sp": "N", "t": "<span class=\"sm-frag\">in bondage inexcusable.<br>For there’s no healing great things with little.’<\/span>"}, {"st": "97", "sp": "N", "t": "<span class=\"sm-src\">Stobaeus Anthology [That Life is short, of little account and full of care]<\/span><span class=\"sm-ctx\">Simonides: [Wil. ascribes to Semonides of Amorgus]<\/span>"}, {"st": "—", "sp": "N", "t": "<span class=\"sm-src\">But there’s one saying of the man of Chios [Homer: cf. Plut. Vit. Horn. 283 Gale, Vit. Hom. Westerm. 28, Cram. A.P. 3. 98. 13]<\/span><span class=\"sm-ctx\">which passes all, ‘The life of man is even as the life of a green leaf’; yet few that receive it with the ear lay it away in the breast; for there’s a hope which springeth in every heart that is young, and so long as man possesseth the flowery bloom of youth there is much that his light heart deems to have no end, counting neither on age nor death, and taking no thought for sickness in time of health. Poor fools they to think so, and not to know that the time of youth and life is but short for such as be mortal! Wherefore be thou wise in time, and fail not when the end is near to give thy soul freely of the best.<\/span>"}, {"st": "98", "sp": "N", "t": "<span class=\"sm-src\">Plutarch Letter of Consolation to Apollonius<\/span><span class=\"sm-ctx\">For as Simonides says:<\/span>"}, {"st": "—", "sp": "N", "t": "<span class=\"sm-frag\">A thousand, aye, ten thousand years are but a point one cannot see, nay the smallest part of a point.<\/span>"}, {"st": "99", "sp": "N", "t": "<span class=\"sm-ctx\">[cf. Plut. Vit,. Thes. 10, Arist. Pol. 2. 1264 a 1]Plutarch Isis and Osiris: I hesitate, lest this be merely, in Simonides’ words, to<\/span>"}, {"st": "—", "sp": "N", "t": "<span class=\"sm-frag\">move what should be left alone, warring against long lapse of time and many tribes and sorts of men<\/span>"}, {"st": "—", "sp": "N", "t": "<span class=\"sm-frag\">who are inspired with the piety they feel towards these Gods.<\/span>"}, {"st": "100", "sp": "N", "t": "<span class=\"sm-ctx\">Stobaeus Selections [That God is the Creator of existing things, and orders all by the word of providence, and what he really is]: Simonides:<\/span>"}, {"st": "—", "sp": "N", "t": "<span class=\"sm-frag\">Great Zeus alone hath the medicines for all ills.<\/span>"}, {"st": "101", "sp": "N", "t": "<span class=\"sm-src\">Athenaeus Doctors at Dinner [on the word ‘to drink’]<\/span><span class=\"sm-ctx\">But my friend, when you have drunk you need not be afraid that the manner of your falling will be backward; for this can never happen to such as drink what Simonides calls<\/span>"}, {"st": "—", "sp": "N", "t": "<span class=\"sm-frag\">wine the defender against care<\/span>"}, {"st": "102", "sp": "N", "t": "<span class=\"sm-src\">The Same [on wines]<\/span><span class=\"sm-ctx\">For in the words of the poet of Ceos:<\/span><span class=\"sm-q\">Nothing that belongeth to Bacchus should be thrown away, nay, not a grapestone.<\/span>"}, {"st": "103", "sp": "N", "t": "<span class=\"sm-src\">The Same [on the cup called σκυφος]<\/span><span class=\"sm-ctx\">Simonides speaks of the <i class=\"sm-qi\">earèd cup<\/i><\/span>"}, {"st": "104", "sp": "N", "t": "<span class=\"sm-src\">Etymologicum Magnum<\/span><span class=\"sm-ctx\">ειριπόνοι δμωαί:<\/span><span class=\"sm-q\">bondwomen that work the wool<\/span><span class=\"sm-ctx\">Simonides, from εριοπονοι with loss of ο; so Herodian Inflexions.<\/span>"}, {"st": "105", "sp": "N", "t": "<span class=\"sm-src\">The Same<\/span><span class=\"sm-ctx\">Alera and Elara: — compare Simonides:<\/span><span class=\"sm-q\">the offspring of Elara<\/span>"}, {"st": "106", "sp": "N", "t": "<span class=\"sm-src\">Etymologicum Gudianum<\/span><span class=\"sm-ctx\">φύξιμος οδμή:a loathsome stench, literally ‘one that makes to flee’; Simonides of Ceos.<\/span>", "hvShift6": [[0, 0], [53, 54]]}, {"st": "—", "sp": "N", "t": "<figure class=\"reader-figure\"><img class=\"reader-figure-img\" src=\"data:image/jpeg;base64,\" alt=\"\" loading=\"lazy\"><figcaption>Pentonkion of Hiero I of Syracuse, Simonides’ final patron<\/figcaption><\/figure>"}, {"st": "107", "sp": "N", "t": "<span class=\"sm-src\">Old Etymologicum Magnum<\/span><span class=\"sm-ctx\">άμιθρησαι ‘to count’: — Simonides says άμιθρός for αριθμός ‘number’ by transposition; compare<\/span>"}, {"st": "—", "sp": "N", "t": "<span class=\"sm-frag\">to number the waves<\/span>"}, {"st": "108", "sp": "N", "t": "<span class=\"sm-src\">Athenaeus Doctors at Dinner [on drinking snow like Simonides]<\/span><span class=\"sm-ctx\">Callistratus in the 7th Book of his Miscellanies relates that once when the poet Simonides was dining out ‘in the season of mighty heat,’ the wine-bearers in mixing snow with the wine for the guests forgot to do so with his, whereupon he improvised the following lines:<\/span>"}, {"st": "—", "sp": "N", "t": "<span class=\"sm-frag\">Of that with which keen Boreas hies him from Thrace to wrap the sides of Olympus and gnaw the hearts of men who have no cloaks, that which thereafter is buried alive in a shroud of Pierian clay, of that let them fill me my share; for ’tis ill bringing to a friend a bumper that is hot.<\/span>"}, {"st": "109", "sp": "N", "t": "<span class=\"sm-src\">Palatine Anthology<\/span><span class=\"sm-ctx\">A dedication to Zeus by Sosus and Soso; by Simonides:<\/span>"}, {"st": "—", "sp": "N", "t": "<span class=\"sm-frag\">Take, Saviour, this from Sosus and Soso,<br>For saving Sosus’ life and Soso woe.<\/span>"}, {"st": "110", "sp": "N", "t": "<span class=\"sm-src\">Athenaeus Doctors at Dinner [on heavy eaters and drinkers]<\/span><span class=\"sm-ctx\">And Timocreon the poet and champion athlete of Rhodes ate and drank his fill, witness his epitaph:<\/span>"}, {"st": "—", "sp": "N", "t": "<span class=\"sm-frag\">Your guttling o’er, your tippling done,<br>You’re lying still, Timocreon.<\/span>"}, {"st": "111", "sp": "N", "t": "<span class=\"sm-ctx\">Palatine Anthology. Simonides; an hexameter and the same arranged as a trochaic tetrameter by transposition of the words:<\/span>"}, {"st": "—", "sp": "N", "t": "<span class=\"sm-frag\">Sing me a song, blest Muse, of the fair-foot Midean’s offspring;<br>Sing me of the fair-foot Midean’s offspring, blessèd Muse, a song.<\/span>"}, {"st": "—", "sp": "N", "t": "<span class=\"sm-note\">[Alcmena’s son Heracles; the first line is prob. a quotation from the opening of a poem by Timocreon of Rhodes, and the whole a skit; cf. Timocr. 10]<\/span>"}, {"st": "112", "sp": "N", "t": "<span class=\"sm-src\">Athenaeus Doctors at Dinner<\/span><span class=\"sm-ctx\">With regard to hares Chamaeleon relates in his book On Simonides that when supping once with Hiero, the poet was left out when the jugged hare was served to the guests, whereupon as Hiero was sending him some, he improvised the following parody:<\/span>"}, {"st": "—", "sp": "N", "t": "<span class=\"sm-frag\">Wide though it was, it could not reach to me.<\/span>"}, {"st": "113,114", "sp": "N", "t": "<span class=\"sm-src\">The Same<\/span><span class=\"sm-ctx\">Verses of the nature of riddles were composed also by Simonides, if we may believe Chamaeleon of Heraclea in his book on that poet; let me quote these:<\/span>"}, {"st": "—", "sp": "N", "t": "<span class=\"sm-frag\">The father of the wayward kid,<br>The child of eve upon each lid,<br>With the fell fish lies jowl by cheek;<br>And so my quest is still to seek.<br>For they refuse their aid to lend<br>Lord Bacchus’ butcher-knight to mend.<\/span>"}, {"st": "—", "sp": "N", "t": "<span class=\"sm-ctx\">Some explain it thus... Others say it was the custom at Iulis that the ox to be sacrificed to Dionysus should be killed with an axe by a boy. The festival being near, the axe had been sent to be repaired, and Simonides, who was then a lad, was sent off to the blacksmith’s to fetch it. Finding the man asleep and his bellows and tongs lying on the ground end to end, he returned to his companions and put to them the above conundrum. For the ‘father of the kid’ is the bellow’s, the ‘fell fish’ the ‘crab’ or tongs, ‘the child of eve’ sleep, and ‘Bacchus’ butcher-knight’ the axe. There is another piece by Simonides which puzzles readers who do not know the story:<\/span>"}, {"st": "—", "sp": "N", "t": "<span class=\"sm-frag\">Who would not be of cricket’s prize the winner,<br>To son of Panopeus shall carry dinner.<\/span>"}, {"st": "—", "sp": "N", "t": "<span class=\"sm-ctx\">Now it is said that the poet used to stay at Carthaea while training the choruses there, and the training-school being high up by the temple of Apollo and far from the sea, all the chorus-men, including the pupils of Simonides, fetched their water from the spring in the lower part of the town. This they did by means of a jackass which they called Epeius after the Epeius who carries water for the Atreidae in the Trojan story, a story which was depicted on the walls of the temple of Apollo near which they were training.... Now it was arranged that any chorister who came late should provide the jackass with a quart of barley. This is what is referred to in the verses; he who would not be winner of the cricket’s prize means he who would not [learn to] sing, the son of Panopeus means the jackass, and the dinner the quart of barley.<\/span>"}, {"st": "115", "sp": "N", "t": "<span class=\"sm-src\">Plutarch On Restraining Anger<\/span><span class=\"sm-ctx\">It seems that Marsyas perforce curbed the violence of his breath by a mouthpiece and cheekbands and thus prevented the ugly puffing-out of his face:<\/span>"}, {"st": "—", "sp": "N", "t": "<span class=\"sm-frag\">And he joined the fringed sides of his head with day-bright gold, and fitted his wanton mouth with backward-bounden thongs; [i. e. when playing the flute]<\/span>"}, {"st": "—", "sp": "N", "t": "<span class=\"sm-frag\">whereas anger, through its unseemly puffing and stretching of the face, makes the voice even more ugly and unpleasant than it would otherwise be.<\/span>"}, {"st": "—", "sp": "HDR", "t": "BOOK XIV. INSCRIPTIONS"}, {"st": "—", "sp": "HDR", "t": "Epitaphs"}, {"st": "116", "sp": "N", "t": "<span class=\"sm-src\">Planudean Anthology<\/span><span class=\"sm-ctx\">Simonides:<\/span>"}, {"st": "—", "sp": "N", "t": "<span class=\"sm-frag\">We were slain in a glen of Dirphys, and the mound of our grave is made beside Euripus at our country’s charge, and rightly so; for by abiding the onset of the cruel cloud of war we lost our lovely time of youth.<\/span>"}, {"st": "—", "sp": "N", "t": "<span class=\"sm-note\">[cloud: the thick of the fight, ref. to operations against Chalcis 500 (?)]<\/span>"}, {"st": "117", "sp": "N", "t": "<span class=\"sm-note\">[cf. Aristid. 2. 511, where 1. 2 runs ‘and put to flight ninety thousand Medes’]<\/span>"}, {"st": "—", "sp": "N", "t": "<span class=\"sm-src\">Lycurgus Speech against Leocrates<\/span><span class=\"sm-ctx\">Thus we may see unimpeachable testimony to their valour engraven upon their tombs for all Greece to read, to the Spartans this: (119); and to your own ancestors this:<\/span>"}, {"st": "—", "sp": "N", "t": "<span class=\"sm-frag\">At Marathon the Athenians fought for Greece and scattered the might of the Mede and all his gold.<\/span>"}, {"st": "118,119,120", "sp": "N", "t": "<span class=\"sm-src\">Herodotus Histories [on the Battle of Thermopylae]<\/span><span class=\"sm-ctx\">They were buried where they fell, and with them the men who died before the withdrawal of those whom Leonidas sent to the rear; and the following inscriptions stand over their graves:<\/span>"}, {"st": "—", "sp": "N", "t": "<span class=\"sm-q\">Here four thousand of the Peloponnese once fought with three thousand thousand.<\/span><span class=\"sm-ctx\">Such is the epitaph of the whole force; of the Spartans in particular this:<\/span><span class=\"sm-q\">Stranger, go tell the Lacedaemonians that we lie here in obedience to their word.<\/span><span class=\"sm-ctx\">And of the seer this:<\/span>"}, {"st": "—", "sp": "N", "t": "<span class=\"sm-frag\">This is the tomb of the famous Megistias, slain by the Medes beside the river Spercheius, the seer who well-knowing that his doom was nigh, would not forsake the leaders of Sparta.<\/span>"}, {"st": "—", "sp": "N", "t": "<span class=\"sm-ctx\">The epitaphs and pillars, with the exception of the epitaph of the seer, were accorded them by the Amphictyons. The epitaph of the seer Megistias was put up by Simonides the son of Leoprepes because of the friendship he bore him. [the evidence of Hdt., who is concerned only with the setting-up of the epitaphs, must not be taken as indicating that S. did not write the first two as well as the third]<\/span>"}, {"st": "121", "sp": "N", "t": "<span class=\"sm-note\">[ascription doubtful]<\/span>"}, {"st": "—", "sp": "N", "t": "<span class=\"sm-src\">Strabo Geography<\/span><span class=\"sm-ctx\">Opus is the mother-city of the Locrians, witness the inscription on the first of the five pillars commemorating Thermopylae, the one near the general grave:<\/span>"}, {"st": "—", "sp": "N", "t": "<span class=\"sm-frag\">Of those who died for the sake of Greece against the Medes these are mourned by Opus the mother-city of the Locrians of the upright laws.<\/span>"}, {"st": "122", "sp": "N", "t": "<span class=\"sm-ctx\">[perh. by Mnasalcas (cf. A P. 13. 21)]Palatine Anthology. The Same (Simonides) on those who died with Leonidas the Spartan:<\/span>"}, {"st": "—", "sp": "N", "t": "<span class=\"sm-frag\">Famous are they this earth doth cover, slain here with thee, Leonidas king of spacious Lacedaemon, when they fought and abode the strength of many and many an arrow and swift-footed horse and man of Media.<\/span>"}, {"st": "123", "sp": "N", "t": "<span class=\"sm-note\">[cf. Plut. Hdt. mal. 39, who apparently does not ascribe it to S. ]<\/span>"}, {"st": "—", "sp": "N", "t": "<span class=\"sm-ctx\">Dio Chrysostom Orations: And they (the Corinthians) fought the best at Salamis and were the prime cause of the victory. For I prefer the witness not of Herodotus but of their tomb and of Simonides, who wrote the following epitaph on the Corinthians who were buried at Salamis:<\/span>"}, {"st": "—", "sp": "N", "t": "<span class=\"sm-frag\">Once, O stranger, we lived in the well-watered citadel of Corinth, but now we dwell in Ajax’ isle of Salamis. [so the stone; literary tradition, i.e.. Plut, and Dio, adds ‘where we saved sacred Greece by taking Phoenician ships with Persians and Medes’]<\/span>"}, {"st": "124", "sp": "N", "t": "<span class=\"sm-src\">Plutarch Malignity of Herodotus [after 123]<\/span><span class=\"sm-ctx\">The cenotaph at the Isthmus bears the following inscription:<\/span>"}, {"st": "—", "sp": "N", "t": "<span class=\"sm-frag\">With our lives saved we all Greece when her fate hung by a single hair.<\/span>"}, {"st": "125", "sp": "N", "t": "<span class=\"sm-src\">The Same [after 124]<\/span><span class=\"sm-ctx\">And indeed Adeimantus himself, whom Herodotus always maligns most bitterly, and particularly in saying that he was the only general who resisted (Themistocles) with a view to making his escape from Artemisium, pray consider what fame he afterwards enjoyed:<\/span>"}, {"st": "—", "sp": "N", "t": "<span class=\"sm-frag\">This is the tomb of that Adeimantus, through whose counsels Greece put on the crown of freedom.<\/span>"}, {"st": "126", "sp": "N", "t": "<span class=\"sm-src\">Palatine Anthology<\/span><span class=\"sm-ctx\">Simonides:<\/span>"}, {"st": "—", "sp": "N", "t": "<span class=\"sm-frag\">These crowned their dear country with fame inextinguishable by wrapping round them the mist and gloom of death; though they died they are not dead, for their valour brings them back in glory from the world below.<\/span>"}, {"st": "127", "sp": "N", "t": "<span class=\"sm-src\">Palatine Anthology<\/span><span class=\"sm-ctx\">Simonides:<\/span>"}, {"st": "—", "sp": "N", "t": "<span class=\"sm-frag\">If the greatest part of virtue is to die well, that hath Fortune given, of all men, unto us; we lie here in glory unaging because we strove to crown Greece with freedom.<\/span>"}, {"st": "128", "sp": "N", "t": "<span class=\"sm-src\">The Same<\/span><span class=\"sm-ctx\">Of uncertain authorship, upon the Athenian vanguard:<\/span>"}, {"st": "—", "sp": "N", "t": "<span class=\"sm-frag\">... the children of the Athenians drove out the Persian host and saved their country from woeful servitude.<\/span>"}, {"st": "—", "sp": "N", "t": "<span class=\"sm-note\">[prob. incomplete; ascription doubtful: ref. to Plataea?]<\/span>"}, {"st": "129", "sp": "N", "t": "<span class=\"sm-src\">The Same<\/span><span class=\"sm-ctx\">Simonides:<\/span>"}, {"st": "—", "sp": "N", "t": "<span class=\"sm-frag\">Because of these men’s manly virtue the smoke of the burning of spacious Tegea has not gone to the sky; for they chose to leave their children a country green and gay with freedom, and themselves to die in the forefront of the battle.<\/span>"}, {"st": "130", "sp": "N", "t": "<span class=\"sm-note\">[B refers these to Plataea, comparing Hdt. 9. 85, but cf. Paus. 9. 2. 5; perh. alternative suggestions for the same monument, both preserved in S.’s collected works]<\/span>"}, {"st": "—", "sp": "N", "t": "<span class=\"sm-src\">The Same<\/span><span class=\"sm-ctx\">Simonides:<\/span>"}, {"st": "—", "sp": "N", "t": "<span class=\"sm-frag\">Let us remember the fair-fighting men whose tomb this is, who died to save the pastures of Tegea, wielding the spear for their country that their dear Greece might not doff freedom from her renowned head.<\/span>"}, {"st": "131", "sp": "N", "t": "<span class=\"sm-src\">Aristides On the Extemporary Addition<\/span><span class=\"sm-ctx\">Do you perchance call such words as these mere boastfulness<\/span>"}, {"st": "—", "sp": "N", "t": "<span class=\"sm-frag\">... and those who died before Byzantium, to save the fishy straits of the sea, men swift in the work of war.<\/span>"}, {"st": "132", "sp": "N", "t": "<span class=\"sm-src\">Palatine Anthology<\/span><span class=\"sm-ctx\">[Simonides - ascription doubtful; cf. Paus. 10. 15. 4] on those who won distinction with Cimon at the Eurymedon:<\/span>"}, {"st": "—", "sp": "N", "t": "<span class=\"sm-frag\">These lost their splendid youth at the Eurymedon, fighting the van of the Median bowmen with the spear; both ashore and on swift shipboard they fought, and left when they died a right goodly memorial of their valour.<\/span>"}, {"st": "133", "sp": "N", "t": "<span class=\"sm-src\">The Same<\/span><span class=\"sm-ctx\">[Simonides] on the Greeks who fell at the Eurymedon:<\/span>"}, {"st": "—", "sp": "N", "t": "<span class=\"sm-frag\">In these men’s breasts the impetuous War-God washed the long-pointed arrow with crimson drops, and instead of javelineers this dust shrouds the living memorials of corpses without life.<\/span>"}, {"st": "134", "sp": "N", "t": "<span class=\"sm-src\">On a stone of the fourth or fifth century A.D. found near Megara<\/span><span class=\"sm-ctx\">The epitaph of the heroes who died in the Persian War and lie where they fell, being decayed through lapse of time, the high priest Helladius caused it to be inscribed to the honour of the fallen and of the city; the author is Simonides:<\/span>"}, {"st": "—", "sp": "N", "t": "<span class=\"sm-frag\">We received the lot of death because we were fain to exalt the day of freedom unto Greece and the Megarians.<\/span>"}, {"st": "—", "sp": "N", "t": "<span class=\"sm-frag\">The city has sacrificed a bull to them every year even to our own time.<\/span>"}, {"st": "135", "sp": "N", "t": "<span class=\"sm-src\">Palatine Anthology<\/span><span class=\"sm-ctx\">[Simonides] on the Athenian vanguard:<\/span>"}, {"st": "—", "sp": "N", "t": "<span class=\"sm-frag\">Farewell, ye glorious princes of war, noble young chivalry of Athens, who lost your youth fighting against the more part of all Greece for the sake of your country of the fair dances. [the stone belongs to the mid-5th cent.; ref. prob. to the Athenian defeat at Tanagra 457 (Thuc. 1. 108); S. died 468]<\/span>"}, {"st": "136", "sp": "N", "t": "<span class=\"sm-src\">The Same<\/span><span class=\"sm-ctx\">Simonides, on certain men who were shipwrecked: [prob. in the Corinthian Gulf on the way to Delphi with an offering from the spoils of the victory at Cumae in 474; the inscr. was on a cenotaph]<\/span>"}, {"st": "—", "sp": "N", "t": "<span class=\"sm-frag\">All these men, on their way to Apollo with first-fruits of the Tyrrhenian spoil, had their burial of one sea, one night, and one ship.<\/span>"}, {"st": "137", "sp": "N", "t": "<span class=\"sm-src\">Palatine Anthology<\/span><span class=\"sm-ctx\">[Simonides] on one Lion who was guarded by a lion of marble:<\/span>"}, {"st": "—", "sp": "N", "t": "<span class=\"sm-frag\">I am the most valiant of beasts, even as he whom now I guard in stone astride this grave was most valiant of men; if Lion had had my name without my nature, then had I never set foot upon this tomb.<\/span>"}, {"st": "—", "sp": "N", "t": "<span class=\"sm-note\">[ascription doubtful; B, who wrongly rejects 3-4 as an addition, sugg. the L. of Hdt. 7. 180; Leonidas (Hdt. 7. 225) impossible, cf. Paus. 3. 8. 2]<\/span>"}, {"st": "138", "sp": "N", "t": "<span class=\"sm-src\">Anonymous Mythographer<\/span><span class=\"sm-ctx\">For it is not true that the soul leaves the body, but rather that the body leaves the soul. Hence both the poet Simonides... and Statius to the same effect in Book VIII of the Thebaid: ‘I hate the limbs and this fragile usage of a body that deserts the soul.’<\/span>"}, {"st": "—", "sp": "N", "t": "<span class=\"sm-frag\">e. g. For the body of such a man as this leaveth I at death his soul, not his soul the body.<\/span>"}, {"st": "139", "sp": "N", "t": "<span class=\"sm-src\">Thucydides History<\/span><span class=\"sm-ctx\">Aiantides the son of Hippoclus tyrant of Lampsacus soon after received Hippias’ daughter Archedicè in marriage, her Athenian father giving her to a Lampsacene because he knew that the rulers of that city had great influence with king Darius. Archedicè’s tomb is at Lampsacus, and bears the following inscription:<\/span>"}, {"st": "—", "sp": "N", "t": "<span class=\"sm-frag\">This dust covers Archedicè the daughter of Hippias the foremost Greek of his time; daughter, wife, sister, and mother, of despots, she lifted not up her heart unto presumptuousness.<\/span>"}, {"st": "140", "sp": "N", "t": "<span class=\"sm-src\">Palatine Anthology<\/span><span class=\"sm-ctx\">Simonides:<\/span>"}, {"st": "—", "sp": "N", "t": "<span class=\"sm-frag\">I shall remember; for she that lies here cannot lie unnamed, to wit Xanthippè the beauteous wife of Archenautes and grandchild’s grandchild of that Periander who gave the word of sovereignty to them of towered Corinth.<\/span>"}, {"st": "—", "sp": "N", "t": "<span class=\"sm-note\">[the speaker is apparently the statue of a Siren or the like]<\/span>"}, {"st": "141", "sp": "N", "t": "<span class=\"sm-src\">The Same<\/span><span class=\"sm-ctx\">By the same (Simonides), on Megacles:<\/span><span class=\"sm-q\">When I behold the tomb of Megacles, I pity you, poor Callias.<\/span><span class=\"sm-note\">[Callias: his father, son of Hipponicus ‘Ammon’ of the Alcmaeonidae?]<\/span>"}, {"st": "142", "sp": "N", "t": "<span class=\"sm-src\">The Same<\/span><span class=\"sm-ctx\">Simonides, on one that was shipwrecked on Geraueia and the Scironian Rocks:<\/span>"}, {"st": "—", "sp": "N", "t": "<span class=\"sm-frag\">Skyey Geraneia, thou evil crag, I would thou lookedst on Ister far away and upon Scythia’s distant Tanais, and that the Scironian Cliff dwelt not beside the sea by which Iphimenes sailed about the rock Moluris; but alas! he is a chill corpse in the deep, and this empty tomb proclaims a bitter seafaring.<\/span>"}, {"st": "143", "sp": "N", "t": "<span class=\"sm-src\">Palatine Anthology<\/span><span class=\"sm-ctx\">By the same (Simonides):<\/span>"}, {"st": "—", "sp": "N", "t": "<span class=\"sm-frag\">When Timomachus was breathing forth his precious youth in his father’s arms, he cried ‘Never will you cease to long, O son of Timenor, for the valour or the virtue of your dear son.’<\/span>"}, {"st": "—", "sp": "N", "t": "<span class=\"sm-note\">[the son’s name is doubtful]<\/span>"}, {"st": "144", "sp": "N", "t": "<span class=\"sm-src\">The Same<\/span><span class=\"sm-ctx\">— [Simonides or] Simias, [more prob. Simias, cf. Ath. 11. 491c] epitaph of a girl named Gorgo:<\/span>"}, {"st": "—", "sp": "N", "t": "<span class=\"sm-frag\">These were the last words of Gorgo as she wept with her dear mother’s arms about her neck: ‘May you remain with my father, and bear, unto a better fortune, another daughter to care for you when you be grey-headed.’<\/span>"}, {"st": "145", "sp": "N", "t": "<span class=\"sm-src\">The Same<\/span><span class=\"sm-ctx\">Simonides, on Timarchus, who died young of an illness:<\/span>"}, {"st": "—", "sp": "N", "t": "<span class=\"sm-frag\">Alas, thou fell disease! Why dost thou grudge the souls of men their sojourn with lovely youth? Now it is the young Timarchus thou hast reft of delicious life, and that ere he might look upon a wedded spouse.<\/span>"}, {"st": "146", "sp": "N", "t": "<span class=\"sm-src\">The Same<\/span><span class=\"sm-ctx\">By the same (Simonides), on Theognis of Sinopè:<\/span>"}, {"st": "—", "sp": "N", "t": "<span class=\"sm-frag\">I am the tomb of Theognis of Sinopè, to whom I was put up by Glaucus in return for a companionship of many years.<\/span>"}, {"st": "147", "sp": "N", "t": "<span class=\"sm-ctx\">Palatine Anthology. By the same (Simonides), on Cleisthenes of Ceos who perished at sea [on a cenotaph]:<\/span>"}, {"st": "—", "sp": "N", "t": "<span class=\"sm-frag\">Your tomb stands on no foreign soil, Cleisthenes, but the doom of death overtaking you in the Euxine Sea you have lost the sweet delicious home coming and returned no more to wave-washed Ceos.<\/span>"}, {"st": "148", "sp": "N", "t": "<span class=\"sm-src\">The Same<\/span><span class=\"sm-ctx\">Simonides, on Cleodemus who was killed by the Thracians:<\/span>"}, {"st": "—", "sp": "N", "t": "<span class=\"sm-frag\">Cleodemus too was led by Honour to a lamentable death, the which he met in a Thracian ambuscade by the mouth of the ever-flowing Theaerus, [perh. the Tearus of Hdt. 4. 90] and the name of his father Diphilus has been made famous by the deeds of his warrior son.<\/span>"}, {"st": "149", "sp": "N", "t": "<span class=\"sm-src\">The Same<\/span><span class=\"sm-ctx\">Simonides, on Nicodicus:<\/span>"}, {"st": "—", "sp": "N", "t": "<span class=\"sm-frag\">Each is hurt by the loss of his own; the loss of Nicodicus hurts both his friends and all this great city.<\/span>"}, {"st": "150", "sp": "N", "t": "<span class=\"sm-src\">The Same<\/span><span class=\"sm-ctx\">Simonides:<\/span>"}, {"st": "—", "sp": "N", "t": "<span class=\"sm-frag\">Some one rejoices that I, Theodorus, am dead; another will rejoice over him; we are all debts due to Death.<\/span>"}, {"st": "151", "sp": "N", "t": "<span class=\"sm-src\">Palatine Anthology<\/span><span class=\"sm-ctx\">Simonides, on Pythonax and his brother:<\/span>"}, {"st": "—", "sp": "N", "t": "<span class=\"sm-frag\">Here lie Pythonax and his brother, ere they might see the full perfecting of lovely youth, and their father Megaristus hath put up an immortal monument in honour of his mortal children dead. [the unnamed brother was peril, an infant]<\/span>"}, {"st": "152", "sp": "N", "t": "<span class=\"sm-ctx\">The Same:<\/span>"}, {"st": "—", "sp": "N", "t": "<span class=\"sm-frag\">Sir, you look not on the tomb of Croesus, but since your poor man needs but a little gravestone it is great enough for me.<\/span>"}, {"st": "153", "sp": "N", "t": "<span class=\"sm-ctx\">The Same:<\/span>"}, {"st": "—", "sp": "N", "t": "<span class=\"sm-frag\">Ere I might behold my bridal bed, I Gorgippus went down to the chamber unescapable of flaxenhaired Persephonè.<\/span>"}, {"st": "154", "sp": "N", "t": "<span class=\"sm-src\">The Same<\/span><span class=\"sm-ctx\">Simonides: an hexameter followed by a pentameter, two trimeters, and an hexameter:<\/span>"}, {"st": "—", "sp": "N", "t": "<span class=\"sm-frag\">Here lies Dandes of Argos, the runner of the single course, after glorifying the horse-breeding land of his birth by two victories at Olympia, three at Delphi, two at the Isthmus, fifteen at Nemea, and others well-nigh past counting.<\/span>"}, {"st": "155", "sp": "N", "t": "<span class=\"sm-src\">Palatine Anthology<\/span><span class=\"sm-ctx\">Simonides:<\/span><span class=\"sm-q\">This tomb did Spinther build for Spinther dead.<\/span><span class=\"sm-note\">[father for son?]<\/span>"}, {"st": "156", "sp": "N", "t": "<span class=\"sm-src\">The Same<\/span><span class=\"sm-ctx\">On Brotachus: Simonides:<\/span><span class=\"sm-q\">I that lie here am Brotachus of Gortyn, a Cretan born, and I came not for this but on business.<\/span>"}, {"st": "157-158", "sp": "N", "t": "<span class=\"sm-src\">The Same<\/span><span class=\"sm-ctx\">Simonides on himself: Simonides, finding a corpse on a certain island, buried it and wrote this epitaph over the grave:<\/span>"}, {"st": "—", "sp": "N", "t": "<span class=\"sm-frag\">Grant, O God of Guest and Host, that those who slew me shall meet with like fortune, but those who put me under the sod may enjoy a happy life.<\/span>"}, {"st": "—", "sp": "N", "t": "<span class=\"sm-ctx\">The ghost of the buried man now appeared to Simonides and urged him not to set sail. His fellow-travellers refusing to take the warning, Simonides stayed behind alone and was saved (from the death which overtook them). Whereupon he put over the grave the following lines:<\/span>"}, {"st": "—", "sp": "N", "t": "<span class=\"sm-ctx\">This is he that saved the life of Simonides of Ceos, he who though dead yet showed his gratitude to the living.<\/span>"}, {"st": "159", "sp": "N", "t": "<span class=\"sm-src\">Pollux Vocabulary<\/span><span class=\"sm-ctx\">Even Lycas the Thessalian hound was made famous by Simonides, who wrote this epitaph over her grave:<\/span>"}, {"st": "—", "sp": "N", "t": "<span class=\"sm-frag\">Methinks, huntress Lycas, even the dead white bones in this tomb must still affright the beasts of the field; for thy valour is well-known to great Pelion and far-seen Ossa and the towering sheep-walks of Cithaeron.<\/span>"}, {"st": "—", "sp": "HDR", "t": "Dedications"}, {"st": "160", "sp": "N", "t": "<span class=\"sm-note\">[cf. Eust. Il. 984. 8]<\/span>"}, {"st": "—", "sp": "N", "t": "<span class=\"sm-src\">Hephaestion Handbook of Metre<\/span><span class=\"sm-ctx\">Every metrical line ends in a complete word; hence such lines as these of Simonides in the Inscriptions are incorrect:<\/span>"}, {"st": "—", "sp": "N", "t": "<span class=\"sm-frag\">A marvellous great light shone upon Athens when Aristogeiton and Harmodius slew Hipparchus.<\/span>"}, {"st": "—", "sp": "N", "t": "<span class=\"sm-note\">[the word Aristogeiton, to come into the metre, has to be divided between the two lines — prob. inscribed beneath the statues of the tyrannicides erected in 477, cf. Mar. Par. 54 (above, p. 248)]<\/span>"}, {"st": "161", "sp": "N", "t": "<span class=\"sm-note\">[date of battles, 507; cf. Diod. Exc. Vat. 3. 40, A.P. 6. 343, Aristid. 2. 512 (after others by S.), Paus. 1. 28. 2, Him. Or. 2. 12]<\/span>"}, {"st": "—", "sp": "N", "t": "<span class=\"sm-src\">Herodotus History<\/span><span class=\"sm-ctx\">The Athenians set up as a tithe of the ransom (of the Boeotian and Chalcidian prisoners) a chariot-and-four in bronze, which stands first on the left as you enter the Propylaea of the Acropolis, and bears the following inscription:<\/span>"}, {"st": "—", "sp": "N", "t": "<span class=\"sm-src\">The sons of Athens, by subduing them with their deeds of war, extinguished in an iron chain [this would recall the actual chains of the ransomed Boeotian and Chalcid an prisoners; dedicated on the Acropolis where Hdt. saw them]<\/span><span class=\"sm-ctx\">of gloom the pride of the nations of Boeotia and Chalcis, whose tithe is here dedicated in these horses unto Pallas.<\/span>"}, {"st": "162", "sp": "N", "t": "<span class=\"sm-src\">Planudean Anthology<\/span><span class=\"sm-ctx\">Simonides:<\/span>"}, {"st": "—", "sp": "N", "t": "<span class=\"sm-frag\">The goat-footed Pan, the Arcadian, the enemy of Media, the ally of Athens, I was dedicated here by Miltiades.<\/span>"}, {"st": "163", "sp": "N", "t": "<span class=\"sm-src\">Plutarch Malignity of Herodotus<\/span><span class=\"sm-ctx\">The offerings dedicated in the temple of Leto by Diodorus, one of the Corinthian commanders, bear the following inscription [ref. to Salamis]:<\/span>"}, {"st": "—", "sp": "N", "t": "<span class=\"sm-frag\">These arms of the Medes whom they sank in the sea the crew of Diodorus dedicated to Leto as a memorial of the sea-fight.<\/span>"}, {"st": "164", "sp": "N", "t": "<span class=\"sm-src\">The Same<\/span><span class=\"sm-ctx\">And then shall we believe what he says about a single individual or state, when in one word he robs Greece of the victory, destroys the trophy, and damns as mere boasting and bluff the inscription the Athenians dedicated to Artemis of the East? The inscription runs thus:<\/span>"}, {"st": "—", "sp": "N", "t": "<span class=\"sm-frag\">When the host of the Mede was destroyed, the sons of Athens defeated tribes of all manner of men from Asia in a fight upon this sea, and dedicated these tokens unto the Virgin Artemis. [ref. to Artemisium]<\/span>"}, {"st": "165", "sp": "N", "t": "<span class=\"sm-src\">The Same<\/span><span class=\"sm-ctx\">It becomes clear that his object in concocting this lie was not to praise Democritus but to shame the Naxians, when we remark that he has passed over without a word the success of Democritus and the distinguished service indicated by the Inscription [more likely an Elegiac poem, but they may have formed a single Book with the Inscriptions] of Simonides:<\/span>"}, {"st": "—", "sp": "N", "t": "<span class=\"sm-frag\">... Democritus, as one of the three that commanded the fight when the Greeks fought the Medes on the sea off Salamis, took five ships from the foe, and for a sixth feat saved Doris [the Peloponnese] from falling into Barbarian hands.<\/span>"}, {"st": "166", "sp": "N", "t": "<span class=\"sm-src\">Scholiast on Pindar [‘Among them blooms Ares in the terrible spears of the young men’]<\/span><span class=\"sm-ctx\">— According to Theopompus, the Corinthian women, too, went to the temple of Aphrodite and prayed to her that their men should be filled with love of the battle for Greece against the Medes... and he declares that the following elegiac poem which was dedicated there is still to be seen on the left hand as you enter the temple: ‘These’ etc.<\/span>"}, {"st": "—", "sp": "N", "t": "<span class=\"sm-src\">Athenaeus Doctors at Dinner<\/span><span class=\"sm-ctx\">According to Chamaeleon of Heraclea in his book On Pindar, it is an ancient custom at Corinth that when the city makes supplication to Aphrodite in any great crisis, the intercession shall be attended by as many as possible of the courtesans, who shall pray to the Goddess and afterwards be present at the sacrifice. Accordingly when Greece was invaded by the Persian, the Corinthian courtesans, if we may believe Theopompus and the 7th Book of Timaeus, went to Aphrodite’s temple and prayed for the salvation of Greece. And thus it was that when the Corinthians dedicated to the Goddess the tablet which is still extant and inscribed on it the name of each of the courtesans who had made that intercession and afterwards attended the sacrifice, the following inscription was dedicated along with it by Simonides: ‘These’ etc.<\/span>"}, {"st": "—", "sp": "N", "t": "<span class=\"sm-src\">Plutarch Malignity of Herodotus<\/span><span class=\"sm-ctx\">And yet the fact that the women of Corinth, and they only of all the women of Greece, made that wonderful and beautiful prayer that the Goddess should fill their men with love of the battle against the barbarians, was probably well-known to the remotest Carian, let alone Herodotus. Indeed it was matter of common talk, and when bronze images were dedicated in the temple of Aphrodite an inscription was composed for them by Simonides to the following effect:<\/span>"}, {"st": "—", "sp": "N", "t": "<span class=\"sm-frag\">These women were dedicated in effigy after that they had made public intercession to the Cyprian for Greece and for their fair-fighting fellow-countrymen, ⟨that she should fill their men with love of the battle against the barbarous-tongued nations of the Medes;⟩ for the mighty Goddess would not deliver over the citadel of Greece into the hands of Median bowmen.<\/span>"}, {"st": "167", "sp": "N", "t": "<span class=\"sm-src\">Thucydides History<\/span><span class=\"sm-ctx\">The Spartan nobles carefully investigated Pausanias’ behaviour to see if he had departed from the established customs, and among other things fastened upon the following personal eulogy which he had seen fit some time before to have inscribed upon the tripod which the Greeks dedicated at Delphi as an offering from the spoil of the Medes (at Plataea):<\/span>"}, {"st": "—", "sp": "N", "t": "<span class=\"sm-frag\">When Pausanias in command of the Greeks destroyed the Median host he dedicated this memorial unto Phoebus.<\/span>"}, {"st": "—", "sp": "N", "t": "<span class=\"sm-frag\">This inscription was now at once erased by the Spartans, who engraved upon the offering the names of all the cities which had set it up after their combined defeat of the Barbarian.<\/span>"}, {"st": "168", "sp": "N", "t": "<span class=\"sm-ctx\">Diodorus of Sicily Historical Library: The Greeks (after Plataea) took a tithe of the spoil and made with it a gold tripod which they dedicated at Delphi with the following inscription:<\/span>"}, {"st": "—", "sp": "N", "t": "<span class=\"sm-frag\">This tripod was dedicated by the saviours of spacious Greece after that they had saved their cities from hateful servitude.<\/span>"}, {"st": "169", "sp": "N", "t": "<span class=\"sm-src\">Plutarch Malignity of Herodotus<\/span><span class=\"sm-ctx\">Finally the Athenians and Spartans wrote and engraved upon the altar (at Plataea):<\/span>"}, {"st": "—", "sp": "N", "t": "<span class=\"sm-frag\">This altar the Greeks did build to be an altar of the Zeus of Freedom common to a free Greece, after that their deeds of triumphant war had driven out the Persians.<\/span>"}, {"st": "170", "sp": "N", "t": "<span class=\"sm-src\">Scholiast on Pindar<\/span><span class=\"sm-ctx\">It is said that Gelo out of regard for his brothers dedicated to the God gold tripods thus inscribed:<\/span>"}, {"st": "—", "sp": "N", "t": "<span class=\"sm-ctx\">I say that Gelo, Hiero, Polyzalus, and Thrasybulus, sons of Deinomenes, dedicated these tripods out of fifty talents and a hundred litres of the gold of Damaretè, [wife of Gelo who gave her name to a coinage generally identified with the fine silver coinage of the period; the couplet is therefore thought to be a later addition; cf. Diod. 11. 20, Ath. 6. 231 e: but perh. we should read ‘of gold in darics’. Homolle Mel. Weil, finding together at Delphi four tripod-bases, two larger (A and B) bearing dedicatory inscriptions of Gelo and [Hiero?] and two smaller (C and D) without inscr., believes the lost pedestal of C and D to have borne these lines; A’s inscr. speaks of a Nikè as well as a tripod, B’s (of apparently later date) of the weight in [talents and] minae; the historians make no mention of Pol. and Thras., perh. because our inscr. had disappeared; prob. A stood first alone to commemorate Himera (480), B, C, 1) were added by Hiero after Cumae (474) prob. in 468 (cf. Bacch. 3. 17); Sitzler Burs. Jahresb. ’07 reads Hiero for Gelo in Sch.; see Poulsen Delphi, Jebb Bacch. 452] being a tithe of the tithe of the booty they had of their victory over the Barbarian nations when they gave a great army to fight beside the Greeks for freedom.<\/span>"}, {"st": "171", "sp": "N", "t": "<span class=\"sm-src\">Aristides On the Four Great Athenians<\/span><span class=\"sm-ctx\">At the river Eurymedon, having won on both elements in the same day, Cimon set up commemorative monuments of fights both by sea and by land, and thus, amazed as the poets were by the earlier deeds of glory done when the Barbarians invaded Greece, one of them nevertheless sang of the later achievements — not indeed of all of them, but of those that were done on a certain single day.<\/span>"}, {"st": "—", "sp": "N", "t": "<span class=\"sm-frag\">Since the day the sea parted Europe from Asia and the impetuous War-God first haunted the cities of mankind, no fairer deed of earthly men ever befel at once on land and sea. For these men first destroyed many of the Medes ashore and then took a hundred ships of the Phoenicians on the sea, ships and shipmen too; and loud were Asia’s laments when she found herself smitten with both of their mightily-warring hands.<\/span>"}, {"st": "—", "sp": "N", "t": "<span class=\"sm-note\">[to do a thing with both hands was to do it earnestly and thoroughly; there is double intention here, the hands being also ‘arms’ military and naval]<\/span>"}, {"st": "172", "sp": "N", "t": "<span class=\"sm-ctx\">[cf. Aesch. Pers. 460, Hdt. 9. 22, 60]Palatine Anthology: Simonides, on a bow and arrows dedicated in the temple of Athena:<\/span>"}, {"st": "—", "sp": "N", "t": "<span class=\"sm-frag\">This bow and its arrows that lie beneath the roof of Athena’s temple their lamentable warfare done, ofttimes amid the mournful mellay bathed themselves in the blood of the man-destroying horsemen of Persia.<\/span>"}, {"st": "173", "sp": "N", "t": "<span class=\"sm-src\">Palatine Anthology<\/span><span class=\"sm-ctx\">A soldier’s offering to Zeus; Simonides:<\/span>"}, {"st": "—", "sp": "N", "t": "<span class=\"sm-frag\">Rest so, thou fine long ash, against the tall pillar, abiding ever sacred to Zeus the Diviner; for thy bronze point is grown old and thou thyself art worn out with much wielding in dreadful war.<\/span>"}, {"st": "174", "sp": "N", "t": "<span class=\"sm-src\">The Same<\/span><span class=\"sm-ctx\">A dedication of the same (Simonides):<\/span>"}, {"st": "—", "sp": "N", "t": "<span class=\"sm-ctx\">Fifty and six, Simonides, were the bulls and the tripods you had won ere you set up this tablet, even so many were the times you mounted the splendid chariot of glorious Victory for teaching a delightful chorus of men.<\/span>"}, {"st": "175", "sp": "N", "t": "<span class=\"sm-src\">Aristides The Extemporary Addition<\/span><span class=\"sm-ctx\">For Simonides could say:<\/span>"}, {"st": "—", "sp": "N", "t": "<span class=\"sm-ctx\">... and I declare that no man can rival in power of memory Simonides the son of Leoprepes, eighty years old though he be.<\/span>"}, {"st": "—", "sp": "N", "t": "<span class=\"sm-ctx\">These are not the words of another man speaking of Simonides, but his own, and moreover he adds the second line to show that it is not a boast of his youthful prime.<\/span>"}, {"st": "176", "sp": "N", "t": "<span class=\"sm-ctx\">Maximus Planudes: Simonides was skilled in all poetry and music, winning prizes from youth to age: compare the inscription:<\/span>"}, {"st": "—", "sp": "N", "t": "<span class=\"sm-src\">When the tribe Antiochis won a fine-wrought tripod, Adeimantus was arch on at Athens, [477 B.C.]<\/span><span class=\"sm-ctx\">Aristides son of Xenophilus was master of the well-taught chorus of fifty men, and the credit for the teaching fell to Simonides son of Leoprepes, being eighty years of age.<\/span>"}, {"st": "177", "sp": "N", "t": "<span class=\"sm-src\">Palatine Anthology<\/span><span class=\"sm-ctx\">[Bacchylides or Simonides]:<\/span>"}, {"st": "—", "sp": "N", "t": "<span class=\"sm-note\">[ascription doubtful (Σιμωνίδης Steph. Byz. Άκαμάντιον): the true author, otherwise unknown, was prob. Antigenes (below)]<\/span>"}, {"st": "—", "sp": "N", "t": "<span class=\"sm-frag\">Oft have the Dionysian Seasons shouted with joy for the ivy-dight dithyramb at the dances of the tribe Acamantis, and shaded the shining locks of skilful singers with ribbons and with choice roses; but they who have made this tripod to be witness to their Bacchic prize are the men who were taught by Antigenes; and right well did Ariston of Argos nurse with outpourings of the sweet breath of his pure flute the delightful voice of Athenians, who were led in their ring of melody by Hipponicus son of Struthon from the chariot of the Graces, the which have bestowed upon his men name and fame and splendid victory by aid of the God and the violet-crownèd Muses.<\/span>"}, {"st": "178", "sp": "N", "t": "<span class=\"sm-src\">Planudean Anthology<\/span><span class=\"sm-ctx\">Simonides:<\/span>"}, {"st": "—", "sp": "N", "t": "<span class=\"sm-frag\">When you look here pray know that this is Theognetus the Olympian boy-victor, the skilful driver of the car of wrestling, fair to behold and his looks not belied by his prowess, the lad that put a crown upon the city of his noble parents.[he won in 476]<\/span>"}, {"st": "179", "sp": "N", "t": "<span class=\"sm-src\">Palatine Anthology<\/span><span class=\"sm-ctx\">Dedication to Hermes by Leocrates, by the same (Simonides):<\/span>"}, {"st": "—", "sp": "N", "t": "<span class=\"sm-frag\">When you dedicated, O Leocrates son of Stroebus, this sculpture unto Hermes, you were not forgotten of the fair-tressed Muses.<\/span>"}, {"st": "—", "sp": "N", "t": "<span class=\"sm-note\">[cf. A.P. after 6. 213, Suid. άγοστω,Quint. 11. 2. 14; so the stone (1st half of 5th cent.): literary tradition adds ‘or of the delightful Academe in whose dell I proclaim your kindness unto him that comes thither’ ]<\/span>"}, {"st": "180", "sp": "N", "t": "<span class=\"sm-src\">The Same<\/span><span class=\"sm-ctx\">Simonides; an hexameter followed alternatively by a comic tetrameter short by two syllables, [trimeter short by one syllable?] and the Archilochian scazon or halting trimeter: [ascription doubtful; S. is more concise and elsewhere uses logaoedic metres only through metrical necessity]<\/span>"}, {"st": "—", "sp": "N", "t": "<span class=\"sm-frag\">In honour of his native place, the sacred city of Athena child of the dark earth, [like the grasshopper, autochthonous, Thuc. 1. 6] these pretty flutes made with the help of Hephaestus were dedicated to Aphrodite by Opis because of his love for the beautiful Bryson.<\/span>"}, {"st": "181", "sp": "N", "t": "<span class=\"sm-src\">Pausanias Description of Greece [on Olympia]<\/span><span class=\"sm-ctx\">Beside the chariot of Gelo is dedicated a statue of Philon, the work of Glaucias of Aegina. For this statue Simonides son of Leoprepes composed the following very clever lines: [because expressed in the usual metre in the simplest possible terms; cf. 182-3]<\/span>"}, {"st": "—", "sp": "N", "t": "<span class=\"sm-frag\">I hail from Corcyra, my name is Philon, I am the son of Glaucus, and I won the boxing twice at Olympia.<\/span>"}, {"st": "182", "sp": "N", "t": "<span class=\"sm-src\">Planudean Anthology<\/span><span class=\"sm-ctx\">The Same (Simonides):<\/span>"}, {"st": "—", "sp": "N", "t": "<span class=\"sm-frag\">At the Isthmus and at Delphi Diophon son of Philon won jump, race, disc, javelin, and wrestling.<\/span>"}, {"st": "—", "sp": "N", "t": "<span class=\"sm-note\">[i. e. the pentathlon or five-event contest]<\/span>"}, {"st": "183", "sp": "N", "t": "<span class=\"sm-src\">The Same<\/span><span class=\"sm-ctx\">Simonides:<\/span><span class=\"sm-q\">Name self, sire, city, victory. Casmylus, Euagoras, Rhodes, boxing at Pytho.<\/span>"}, {"st": "184", "sp": "N", "t": "<span class=\"sm-src\">Palatine Anthology<\/span><span class=\"sm-ctx\">Simonides; an hexameter followed by a nine-syllable:<\/span>"}, {"st": "—", "sp": "N", "t": "<span class=\"sm-frag\">This statue is the offering of Nicoladas of Corinth, who won the footrace at Delphi, who took sixty jars of oil in five prizes at the Panathenaea, the number of jars varied with the importance of the event] whose rivals thrice upon end came not even before the oath-offerings at holy Isthmus; who won thrice at Nemea, four times at Pellene, twice at Lycaeus, and at Tegea, Aegina, and rocky Epidaurus, at Thebes and at Megara town; who at Phlius won the short course and the contest-of-five, and made great Corinth to rejoice.<\/span>"}, {"st": "185", "sp": "N", "t": "<span class=\"sm-src\">Planudean Anthology<\/span><span class=\"sm-ctx\">The Same (Simonides):<\/span>"}, {"st": "—", "sp": "N", "t": "<span class=\"sm-frag\">This is the noble statue of the noble Milon, who kept his feet victoriously six times by the stream of Pisa.<\/span>"}, {"st": "—", "sp": "N", "t": "<span class=\"sm-note\">[kept his feet: the Gk. is ‘did not fall to his knees’ i.e.. wrestling at Olympia; prob. the stream is intended and not the town]<\/span>"}, {"st": "186", "sp": "N", "t": "<span class=\"sm-src\">Diogenes Laertius Lives of the Philosophers<\/span><span class=\"sm-ctx\">There have been three other men of the name of Arcesilaüs;... and another a statuary, to whom Simonides wrote the following inscription:<\/span>"}, {"st": "—", "sp": "N", "t": "<span class=\"sm-frag\">This is the statue of Artemis, the price of it two hundred Parian drachmas stamped with the goat, and the master that made it with aid of Athena was Arcesilaüs of Oaxus, son of Aristodicus.<\/span>"}, {"st": "187", "sp": "N", "t": "<span class=\"sm-src\">Palatine Anthology<\/span><span class=\"sm-ctx\">Simonides:<\/span>"}, {"st": "—", "sp": "N", "t": "<span class=\"sm-frag\">From Alcon of Crete, the son of Didymus, this wreath to Phoebus for the victory in boxing at the Isthmus.<\/span>"}, {"st": "188", "sp": "N", "t": "<span class=\"sm-src\">Tryphon [on hyperbaton]<\/span><span class=\"sm-ctx\">— Some writers have used hyperbaton or transposition even in syllables, for instance Simonides in the Inscriptions:<\/span>"}, {"st": "—", "sp": "N", "t": "<span class=\"sm-frag\">This statue of Hermes was dedicated in the porch by Demetrius after that he had won at Megara uprightly indeed, but upright, no;<\/span>"}, {"st": "—", "sp": "N", "t": "<span class=\"sm-frag\">where ‘upright no’ is for ‘not upright.’<\/span>"}, {"st": "189", "sp": "N", "t": "<span class=\"sm-src\">Plutarch Life of Themistocles<\/span><span class=\"sm-ctx\">But it is clear that Themistocles was connected by birth with the Lycomids, because the place of initiation at Phlya, which belonged to them and was burnt down by the Persians, was restored by him and adorned with paintings, as we are told by Simonides. [prob. in a dedicatory inscription for the building]<\/span>"}, {"st": "190", "sp": "N", "t": "<span class=\"sm-src\">Pausanias Description of Greece [on a painting by Polygnotus at Delphi]<\/span><span class=\"sm-ctx\">In this part of the painting there is a couplet of Simonides:<\/span>"}, {"st": "—", "sp": "N", "t": "<span class=\"sm-frag\">Polygnotus of Thasos, son of Aglaophon, painted the sack of the citadel of Troy.<\/span>"}, {"st": "191", "sp": "N", "t": "<span class=\"sm-ctx\">Palatine Anthology:<\/span>"}, {"st": "—", "sp": "N", "t": "<span class=\"sm-frag\">This was painted by Iphion of Corinth, and small blame to his hand; for work is far better than fame.<\/span>"}, {"st": "192", "sp": "N", "t": "<span class=\"sm-src\">The Same<\/span><span class=\"sm-ctx\">On the doors; by the same (Simonides):<\/span>"}, {"st": "—", "sp": "N", "t": "<span class=\"sm-frag\">The right hand of these doors did Micon paint,<br>The right as you come out, Dionysius.<\/span>"}, {"st": "193", "sp": "N", "t": "<span class=\"sm-ctx\">Aristotle Rhetoric:<\/span>"}, {"st": "—", "sp": "N", "t": "<span class=\"sm-ctx\">For if an action exceeds what is expected of the actor’s powers or years, or surpasses the common standard of those whom he resembles; or if it is performed in a certain way or at a certain time or at a certain place, it will acquire thereby a greatness, be it of beauty, of excellence, of justice, or of their opposites. Hence the inscription for the statue of the Olympian victor:<\/span>"}, {"st": "—", "sp": "N", "t": "<span class=\"sm-frag\">In the old days with a galling basket on my shoulders I used to carry fish from Argos to Tegea, [but now... ]<\/span>"}, {"st": "—", "sp": "N", "t": "<span class=\"sm-note\">[the lost second couplet doubtless contained name and victory]<\/span>"}, {"st": "194", "sp": "N", "t": "<span class=\"sm-src\">Palatine Anthology<\/span><span class=\"sm-ctx\">Dedication by Simonides:<\/span>"}, {"st": "—", "sp": "N", "t": "<span class=\"sm-frag\">Pray that your gifts, O Cyton, may bring even as full joy to the holy Son of Leto who rules the marketplace of the fair dances, as the praise you have both of stranger and them of Corinth and the crown of thanks that has fallen to your lot.<\/span>"}, {"st": "—", "sp": "N", "t": "<span class=\"sm-note\">[prob. ref. to the voting of a crown by the people for services to the state, this being the inscr. written upon it when he dedicated it to Apollo]<\/span>"}, {"st": "195", "sp": "N", "t": "<span class=\"sm-src\">Herodian Words without Parallel [on ην ‘I was’]<\/span><span class=\"sm-ctx\">Speakers of Attic say ην εγω ‘I was’ and ην εκεινος ‘he was,’ and Simonides uses the same form for the first person plural, as for instance in the Inscriptions:<\/span>"}, {"st": "—", "sp": "N", "t": "<span class=\"sm-frag\">We were once a hundred cups belonging to either city<\/span>"}, {"st": "—", "sp": "N", "t": "<span class=\"sm-note\">[Sparta and Athens?— ‘but now we are united into one tripod, as an offering to the God’? Ludwich]<\/span>"}, {"st": "—", "sp": "N", "t": "<span class=\"sm-frag\">— ην for ημεν.<\/span>"}, {"st": "196", "sp": "N", "t": "<span class=\"sm-src\">Scholiast on Pindar [‘Eileithuia that sittest beside the Fates’]<\/span><span class=\"sm-ctx\">.. Aristodemus the pupil of Aristarchus gives a better account Sogenes [the winning boy] was born to Thearion when he was advanced in years, in answer to a prayer to the Goddess, and so the birth of his son was as it were a favour of Eileithuia. Thus it is owing to the peculiar circumstances of the athlete’s birth that the poet thus addresses the Goddess. Aristodemus’ view was based on an Inscription of Simonides.<\/span>"}, {"st": "197", "sp": "N", "t": "<span class=\"sm-src\">Athenaeus Doctors at Dinner<\/span><span class=\"sm-ctx\">χορωνόν ‘wreath’: Apion in his book On the Latin Language declares that what is now called στέφανος was anciently known as χορωνόν because it was used by the χορευταί or chorus-men in the theatres, who both wore a wreath themselves and competed for one; compare this passage in the Inscriptions of Simonides, where the word χορωνόν occurs:<\/span>"}, {"st": "—", "sp": "N", "t": "<span class=\"sm-frag\">Phoebus, who leads the singing of the children of Tyndareus, is crowned with a wreath by these crickets of ours.<\/span>"}, {"st": "198", "sp": "N", "t": "<span class=\"sm-src\">Athenaeus Doctors at Dinner<\/span><span class=\"sm-ctx\">... a subject painted in the Portico of the Polemarch at Phlius by Sillax of Rhegium, who is mentioned by Epicharmus and Simonides.<\/span>"}, {"st": "199", "sp": "N", "t": "<span class=\"sm-src\">Stobaeus Selections [on Time’s nature and parts and of how many things he is the cause]<\/span><span class=\"sm-ctx\">From the Inscriptions of Simonides:<\/span>"}, {"st": "—", "sp": "N", "t": "<span class=\"sm-frag\">The greatest touchstone of any work is Time, who showeth even the heart of a man beneath his breast.<\/span>"}, {"st": "200", "sp": "N", "t": "<span class=\"sm-src\">Stobaeus Selections [on Time’s nature and parts and of how many things he is the cause]<\/span><span class=\"sm-ctx\">From the Inscriptions of Simonides:<\/span>"}, {"st": "—", "sp": "N", "t": "<span class=\"sm-frag\">... sharp-toothed Time grindeth all things up, aye, even the mightiest.<\/span>"}, {"st": "201", "sp": "N", "t": "<span class=\"sm-src\">Strabo Geography [the Indians]<\/span><span class=\"sm-ctx\">The Noseless are declared by Megasthenes to be gluttons, eaters of raw flesh, and shortlived, dying before they become old; moreover their upper lips are remarkably protruding. Of the Hyperboreans who live for a thousand years he gives the same account as Simonides, Pindar, and other mythologers.<\/span>"}, {"st": "202", "sp": "N", "t": "<span class=\"sm-src\">The Same [the Perrhaebians]<\/span><span class=\"sm-ctx\">Owing to their living side by side in the same district, Simonides gives the names Perrhaebian and Lapith to all the Pelasgiots inhabiting the eastward parts about Gyrton, the mouth of the Peneius, and Mounts Ossa and Pelion, as well as the district of Demetrias and the townships of the plain, Larissa, Crannon, Scotussa, Mopsium, Atrax, and the country of the Nessonian and Boebian Lakes.<\/span>"}, {"st": "203", "sp": "N", "t": "<span class=\"sm-src\">Scholiast on Theocritus [‘Nisaean Megarians, masters of the oar’]<\/span><span class=\"sm-ctx\">(1) Because they are a nautical people, as Simonides testifies; (2) Simonides too praises the Megarians.<\/span>"}, {"st": "204", "sp": "N", "t": "<span class=\"sm-note\">[cf. Suid. s v. (‘proverbial of those who laugh at their own destruction’)]<\/span>"}, {"st": "—", "sp": "N", "t": "<span class=\"sm-src\">Photius Lexicon<\/span><span class=\"sm-ctx\">A Sardonic smile: ... According to Simonides, Talos, the man that Hephaestus made with his hands, took the Sardinians, who refused to carry him over to Minos, and leapt down with them into the fire, as he well might do being made of bronze, and there hugged them to his breast and slew them all grinning upon him.<\/span>"}, {"st": "—", "sp": "N", "t": "<span class=\"sm-src\">Zenobius Proverbs<\/span><span class=\"sm-ctx\">According to Simonides, Talos before he went to Crete lived in Sardinia, and killed many of the inhabitants, who as they died grinned, whence the phrase ‘a Sardonic smile.’<\/span>"}, {"st": "205", "sp": "N", "t": "<span class=\"sm-src\">Philodemus On Piety [Atlas]<\/span><span class=\"sm-ctx\">Simonides represents him as carrying the sky on his shoulders, while in Hesiod [Theog. 517] he ‘holds the broad sky through mighty Necessity, standing erect with head and hands unwearying.’<\/span>"}, {"st": "206", "sp": "N", "t": "<span class=\"sm-note\">[cf. Serv. Verg. A en. 6. 576, 7. 658]<\/span>"}, {"st": "—", "sp": "N", "t": "<span class=\"sm-src\">Scholiast on Hesiod<\/span><span class=\"sm-ctx\">According to Alcaeus the Hydra had nine heads, according to Simonides fifty.<\/span>"}, {"st": "207", "sp": "N", "t": "<span class=\"sm-src\">Scholiast on Apollonius of Rhodes Argonautica [‘being of Ephyra by birth’]<\/span><span class=\"sm-ctx\">Ephyra means Corinth, from Ephyra daughter of Epimetheus, though according to Eumelus and Simonides she was daughter of Oceanus and Tethys, and wife of Epimetheus.<\/span>"}, {"st": "208", "sp": "N", "t": "<span class=\"sm-src\">Scholiast on Euripides Orestes<\/span><span class=\"sm-ctx\">It is clear that the scene of the play is laid at Argos, whereas Homer puts Agamemnon’s palace at Mycenae, and Stesichorus and Simonides at Sparta.<\/span>"}, {"st": "209", "sp": "N", "t": "<span class=\"sm-src\">Scholiast on the Iliad [‘As Idomeneus strode away, Deiphobus cast at him his shining javelin<\/span><span class=\"sm-ctx\">for he had ever an abiding hatred of him’]: As his rival for the love of Helen, witness Ibycus and Simonides.<\/span>"}, {"st": "210", "sp": "N", "t": "<span class=\"sm-src\">[Longinus] On the Sublime [on ‘images’]<\/span><span class=\"sm-ctx\">The imagery of Sophocles is of supreme excellence where the dying Oedipus goes to his grave amidst a gathering tempest, and also where, as the Greeks set sail from Troy, Achilles appears to them over his tomb; and yet the latter image could hardly be more vividly employed than it is by Simonides. But it is impracticable to quote every case in point.<\/span>"}, {"st": "211", "sp": "N", "t": "<span class=\"sm-ctx\">Scholiast on the Iliad [‘Nastes and Amphimachus, the glorious children of Nomion; and he came moreover to the war with, a gold trinket like a girl’]: The marginal mark is because ‘he’ refers to Amphimachus, though Simonides took it of Nastes and because the meaning is not that he came with golden arms, as again Simonides would have it, but with a gold ornament.<\/span>"}, {"st": "212", "sp": "N", "t": "<span class=\"sm-src\">Scholiast on Pindar [‘Among the Corinthians is the sweet-breathèd Muse’]<\/span><span class=\"sm-ctx\">that is, they are musical; for Corinth had very famous poets, including Arion, who is mentioned by Simonides.<\/span>"}, {"st": "213", "sp": "N", "t": "<span class=\"sm-src\">Scholiast on the Iliad [‘daughter of Marpessa’]<\/span><span class=\"sm-ctx\">Idas, the reputed son of Aphareus but true son of Poseidon, by race a Lacedaemonian, desiring a wife, went to Ortygia in Chalcis and carried away thence Euenus’ daughter Marpessa, travelling speedily with horses of Poseidon. Going forth to seek his daughter, Euenus came to the river Lycormas in Aetolia and there sank down; whence the Lycormas came to be known as the Euenus. But nigh to Arenè Idas was met by Apollo, who laid hold on Marpessa, whereat Idas stretched bow and began to fight him for his bride. Then became Zeus judge between them, and bade Marpessa choose her man; when for fear Apollo would leave her when she grew old, she chose Idas. Such is Simonides’ elaboration of the story.<\/span>"}, {"st": "214", "sp": "N", "t": "<span class=\"sm-ctx\">Plut. Life of Lycurgus: Nevertheless, although History is at a loss, we will try to base our account of the man upon such of the recorded facts as are least controverted or have the support of the best authorities. According to the poet Simonides, the father of Lycurgus was not Eunomus, but Lycurgus and Eunomus were sons of Prytanis. This is contrary to most of the authorities, etc.<\/span>"}, {"st": "215", "sp": "N", "t": "<span class=\"sm-src\">The Same Life of Agesilaus<\/span><span class=\"sm-ctx\">And this is why Simonides is said to have called Spartabreaker-in of men<\/span>"}, {"st": "—", "sp": "N", "t": "<span class=\"sm-frag\">as, more than any other city, making her people obedient through her customs, like horses whose breaking-in is begun from the outset.<\/span>"}, {"st": "216", "sp": "N", "t": "<span class=\"sm-src\">Scholiast on Euripides Rhesus [‘the four-part watch of night’]<\/span><span class=\"sm-ctx\">The ancients divide the night into three watches... but Stesichorus and Simonides make it of five watches<\/span>"}, {"st": "217", "sp": "N", "t": "<span class=\"sm-ctx\">Scholiast on Pindar [‘on the mothers side from Astydameia and so sons of Amyntor’ ]... Pindar here makes her a daughter of Amyntor, but according to Hesiod and Simonides her father was Ormenus.<\/span>"}, {"st": "218", "sp": "N", "t": "<span class=\"sm-src\">Stephanus of Byzantium Lexicon<\/span><span class=\"sm-ctx\">Acanthus: — ... there is also an Acanthus in Athamania. The ethnic adjective is Acanthius ‘Acanthian,’ whence the proverb ‘Acanthian cricket’ of taciturn people; for according to Simonides the crickets of that country do not chirp.<\/span>"}, {"st": "219", "sp": "N", "t": "<span class=\"sm-src\">Herodian On Solecism<\/span><span class=\"sm-ctx\">compare <i class=\"sm-qi\">I laughs<\/i><\/span>"}, {"st": "—", "sp": "N", "t": "<span class=\"sm-note\">[if H. is not deceived by a false reading or explanation (which is quite possible), S. must have been joking (perh. in a riddle)]<\/span>"}, {"st": "—", "sp": "N", "t": "<span class=\"sm-ctx\">in the lyric poet Simonides; for ‘I’ is of the first person and ‘laughs’ of the third, and so he has made a false concord, whereas he should have said ‘I laugh’ or ‘he laughs.’<\/span>"}, {"st": "220", "sp": "N", "t": "<span class=\"sm-note\">[cf. Suid., Philem. 141, Phot. (Reitz.) 96]<\/span><span class=\"sm-q\">Zonaras Lexicon άμύνεσθαι ‘to avenge oneself’<br>requite<\/span><span class=\"sm-ctx\">.. used by Simonides for ‘to repay a favour.’<\/span>"}, {"st": "221", "sp": "N", "t": "<span class=\"sm-src\">Athenaeus Doctors at Dinner [on word-coining]<\/span><span class=\"sm-ctx\">I know the poet Simonides somewhere calls Zeus <i class=\"sm-qi\">best-ruler<\/i><\/span>"}, {"st": "222", "sp": "N", "t": "<span class=\"sm-src\">Scholiast on the Iliad [‘fair black-mounted swords’]<\/span><span class=\"sm-ctx\">that is, ‘iron-bound’... ‘having iron handles bound to them’... The handle is called the ‘bond’ or<\/span>"}, {"st": "—", "sp": "N", "t": "<span class=\"sm-ctx\"><i class=\"sm-qi\">binding<\/i> by Simonides.<\/span>"}, {"st": "223", "sp": "N", "t": "<span class=\"sm-src\">The Same [‘astonishment held them as they looked on’]<\/span><span class=\"sm-ctx\">θαμβός <i class=\"sm-qi\">astonishment<\/i> is neuter in Homer, but masculine in Simonides.<\/span>"}, {"st": "224", "sp": "N", "t": "<span class=\"sm-src\">Scholiast on the Iliad [‘his comrades the dearest and most cherished of all’]<\/span><span class=\"sm-ctx\">κεδνότατοι really means ‘wisest,’ but Simonides calls friends κεδνοί<\/span>"}, {"st": "—", "sp": "N", "t": "<span class=\"sm-frag\">cherished<\/span>"}, {"st": "225", "sp": "N", "t": "<span class=\"sm-src\">The Same [‘coffers’]<\/span><span class=\"sm-ctx\">Aristarchus says that the word κιβωτόs <i class=\"sm-qi\">chest<\/i> is modern, not knowing that it occurs in Simonides and Hecataeus.<\/span>"}, {"st": "226", "sp": "N", "t": "<span class=\"sm-src\">Cramer Inedita (Oxford)<\/span><span class=\"sm-ctx\">Disyllables in -τος which do not change their form and have ρ in the penultimate are accented paroxytone, for instance κυρτος ‘weel,’ Mυρτος the city Myrtus, σκίρτος<\/span>"}, {"st": "—", "sp": "N", "t": "<span class=\"sm-ctx\"><i class=\"sm-qi\">frisky<\/i> a word used by Simonides.<\/span>"}, {"st": "227", "sp": "N", "t": "<span class=\"sm-src\">Etymologicum Magnum πρώρα ‘prow’<\/span><span class=\"sm-ctx\">— With the ι... by the derivation from προϊεναι ‘to go forward,’ [the etymology is hardly correct] and by ‘separation,’ since it is found with the two vowels ‘separated,’ as in Homer κυανοπρωίρους and in Simonides κυανοπρώϊρα<\/span>"}, {"st": "—", "sp": "N", "t": "<span class=\"sm-frag\">blue-prowed<\/span>"}, {"st": "—", "sp": "N", "t": "<span class=\"sm-ctx\">Some authorities write the latter half of the compound with an ι, as from -πρωρα with ‘separation’ of ι from ω, but Herodian writes it with the diphthong ει on the pattern of words in -ειρα.<\/span>"}, {"st": "228", "sp": "N", "t": "<span class=\"sm-note\">[cf. Anacr. 80]<\/span><span class=\"sm-src\">Tzetzes on Hesiod [‘a woman... babbling cozeningly’]<\/span><span class=\"sm-ctx\">Babbling:—’ speaking much’;<\/span><span class=\"sm-ctx\"><i class=\"sm-qi\">babbling<\/i> is an epithet of the swallow in Anacreon and Simonides because it is garrulous.<\/span>"}, {"st": "229", "sp": "N", "t": "<span class=\"sm-src\">Hesychius Lexicon νεαρησιν ‘ίπποι!<\/span><span class=\"sm-ctx\">‘Horses from Neaera,’ [the explanation is prob. wrong; the word is a special fern, of νιος ‘young,’ cf. πρίσβειρα] a place in Lemnos; Simonides uses the phrase νεαιραν γνάθον<\/span>"}, {"st": "—", "sp": "N", "t": "<span class=\"sm-frag\">youthful cheek<\/span>"}, {"st": "230", "sp": "N", "t": "<span class=\"sm-ctx\">The Same:<\/span><span class=\"sm-ctx\"><i class=\"sm-qi\">Oeciades<\/i> ‘Son of Oeceus’: Simonides; he was the father of Dexamenus and Hipponoiis.<\/span>"}, {"st": "231", "sp": "N", "t": "<span class=\"sm-src\">Old Etymologicum Magnum, υιος ‘son’<\/span><span class=\"sm-ctx\">— It is nominative υος, genitive υιος, like ιφις ‘snake,’ genitive οφιος; the nominative occurs in Simonides with ‘synaeresis’ or coalescing of the ι and the υ into the diphthong υι, υις<\/span>"}, {"st": "—", "sp": "N", "t": "<span class=\"sm-ctx\"><i class=\"sm-qi\">son;<\/i> the genitive is υιος, accented acute on the first syllable, etc.<\/span>"}, {"st": "—", "sp": "N", "t": "<span class=\"sm-src\">Scholiast on Dionysius of Thrace<\/span><span class=\"sm-ctx\">If a syllable ends with a consonant the next to it will not begin with a ζ, unless the word be foreign like Ariobarzanes or a compound like the μελάνζοφος<\/span>"}, {"st": "—", "sp": "N", "t": "<span class=\"sm-ctx\"><i class=\"sm-qi\">pitch-dark<\/i> of Simonides.<\/span>"}],[]);})();